                    [
                        'heading' => 'Singing in tune with a tuner',
                        'body' => [
                            'Pick Voice, sing an easy "ah" on one note and hold it for a few seconds. Watch your pitch over time rather than the needle: a line that sags at the end of a breath, or starts under the note and slides up to it, shows you a habit worth working on.',
                            'Then turn it round: sing the note first, decide whether you think you\'re flat or sharp, and only then look. That way the tuner trains your ear instead of standing in for it.',
                            'In a choir, use a tuner for single notes, not chords. It measures against the piano\'s equal temperament, while a choir singing a chord in tune leans toward pure intervals: the third of a major chord rings about 14 cents lower than the tuner\'s, and the fifth about 2 cents higher. So listen to the chord, and use the tuner to check your starting note and your long held ones.',
                        ],
                    ],
