            'tuning-forks' => [
                'icon' => 'spark',
                'eyebrow' => 'Pure tones · 7 forks and more',
                'name' => 'Tuning forks',
                'summary' => 'A440, A432, C256 and more: strike one and hear a pure tone ring.',
                'tie' => 'You\'ve heard a pure A. Now find your own home note.',
                'tag' => 'A440, A432, C256 and more',
                'group' => 'tune',
                'title' => 'Online Tuning Fork: A440, A432, C256, C512',
                'description' => 'Strike a virtual tuning fork: A440, A442, A415, A432, C256, C512 and more, as pure tones that ring and fade. Free, no account, works offline.',
                'headline' => 'Online tuning forks',
                'lead' => 'Tap a fork to strike it, hold it to keep it ringing, strike two to hear them beat.',
                'how' => [
                    'Tap the big fork to strike it: it rings and fades like a real one. Hold it to keep it ringing, and let go to let it ring out. Pick another fork from the row under it, and it strikes too.',
                    'To keep it ringing with your hands free, open More and turn on Keep a fork ringing: it sounds until you tap it again. More also has forks most people never need, your own fork at any Hz with its nearest note, and ringing several together to hear them beat.',
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['What frequency is a standard tuning fork?', 'A440: the A above middle C at 440 Hz. It\'s the international standard pitch (ISO 16) and the fork most musicians mean.'],
                    ['How do you tune with a tuning fork?', 'Strike the fork and play or sing the same note. If they\'re a little apart you hear a slow wobble, called beating. Adjust until it slows and stops. Hold the fork here to keep it ringing while you listen.'],
                    ['What is the difference between 432 Hz and 440 Hz?', 'A432 is about a third of a semitone lower than A440. It\'s a real fork, and some people prefer it. Claims that 432 Hz is healthier or more natural have no good evidence behind them; what matters for a group is that everyone uses the same A.'],
                    ['Which fork should I use?', 'A440 for almost everything: it\'s concert pitch, and what tuners and pianos use. A442 matches the many orchestras that tune a little higher, and A415 is for baroque music on period instruments.'],
                    ['What is C256, and why C512?', 'C256 is "scientific" or "philosophical" pitch, where middle C is a power of two. It isn\'t A440\'s middle C (261.63 Hz): it implies A at about 430.5 Hz. C512 is the C above it, the classic fork for the Rinne and Weber hearing tests.'],
                    ['Why do baroque groups tune to A415, and orchestras to A442?', 'Pitch in much of the baroque era was lower than today, and A415, about a semitone below A440, became the usual pitch for period instruments. Many orchestras tune a little above 440, often to 442, which many players say sounds a little brighter.'],
                    ['Why can\'t I hear the low forks on my phone?', 'A phone\'s small speaker barely plays a pure tone as low as C128 or C256. Use headphones for the true tone, or turn on Speaker boost under More, which adds the octave above so it carries.'],
                    ['Can I strike my own frequency?', 'Yes. Under More, type any frequency from 20 to 4000 Hz and strike it. It names the nearest note against A440, and how many cents sharp or flat your fork is.'],
                    ['Tuning fork, pitch pipe or tuner: which is best for tuning?', 'A fork gives one pure, steady reference to tune to by ear. A tuner shows you how far off you are. A pitch pipe gives any starting note. For an instrument a tuner is quickest; to train your ear, a fork.'],
                    ['Can I use this for a hearing or medical test?', 'No. It\'s a musical reference, not a medical device: a phone can\'t match a real fork\'s level or vibration, and those tests are a clinician\'s job.'],
                ],
                // Below the tool: what's worth knowing around it (docs/tools/seo.md), with one reference table.
                'guide' => [
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Tuning fork frequencies',
                        'body' => [
                            'Every fork here, with the note it gives and who uses it. A fork is named by its note and its frequency in Hz: A440 is an A at exactly 440 Hz.',
                        ],
                        'table' => [
                            'head' => ['Fork', 'Note', 'Who uses it'],
                            'rows' => [
                                ['A440', 'A4', 'Concert pitch, the international standard'],
                                ['A442', 'A4', 'Many European orchestras'],
                                ['A443', 'A4', 'Some orchestras that tune higher still'],
                                ['A415', 'A4', 'Baroque music on period instruments'],
                                ['A432', 'A4', 'An alternative some people prefer'],
                                ['A435', 'A4', 'France\'s standard pitch from 1859'],
                                ['A466', 'A4', 'A high baroque church pitch'],
                                ['C256', 'C4', '"Scientific" pitch, middle C a power of two'],
