            'four-part-harmony' => [
                'icon' => 'users',
                'name' => 'Four-part harmony',
                'eyebrow' => 'SATB · voice leading · on a staff',
                'summary' => 'Type chords and get soprano, alto, tenor and bass to sing.',
                'tie' => 'You\'ve voiced the chord. Now sing all four parts yourself.',
                'tag' => 'Voice a chord for four singers',
                'group' => 'harmony',
                // Search words (docs/tools/competitors-transposer-satb.md, section 2): "four part harmony" and "SATB" in the title, short enough to keep " | Stackapella".
                'title' => 'Four-Part Harmony Generator: SATB Voicings',
                'description' => 'Type a chord progression and get four singable parts, soprano, alto, tenor and bass, with smooth voice leading, on a staff you can hear. Free, no account.',
                'headline' => 'Four-part harmony generator',
                'lead' => 'Type the chords, and get four parts a choir can sing: a smooth soprano line, and alto, tenor and bass voiced the way a choral arranger would, with no parallel fifths or octaves and every chord with its third. See them on a staff, hear them together, or hear one part alone to learn it. Free, with no account.',
                'how' => [
                    'Type your chords in the box, like C Am F G7 C. Bar lines, commas and slash chords like G/B are fine.',
                    'Press Play to hear the four parts, and tap a part\'s name to leave it out: hear the others and sing that part yourself.',
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['What is SATB?', 'Soprano, alto, tenor and bass: the four parts of a mixed choir, from highest to lowest. Sopranos and altos are usually women\'s and children\'s voices, tenors and basses men\'s. Four-part harmony gives each part one note of every chord.'],
                    ['What are the voice-leading rules?', 'The ones taught in every harmony class: each part moves as little as it can, by step where possible; no two parts move in parallel fifths or octaves; parts don\'t cross; the upper three stay within an octave of each other; every chord has its third; and the leading tone rises to the tonic, while a seventh falls by step. The generator follows all of them.'],
                    ['Why avoid parallel fifths and octaves?', 'Two parts moving together a fifth or an octave apart blend so well that they sound like one part, and the harmony thins from four voices to three. Keeping them apart keeps each line its own.'],
                    ['What range does each part sing?', 'Roughly: soprano from middle C to A5, alto from F3 to D5, tenor from C3 to A4, and bass from E2 to E4. The generator keeps each part in an easy middle range, so most choirs can sing it as written.'],
                    ['What does a slash chord like G/B mean?', 'Play or sing G, with B in the bass. The generator keeps that bass note, so a line like C, G/B, Am walks down step by step, as written.'],
                    ['Can I hear one part alone?', 'Yes. Tap a part\'s name under the staff to leave it out, then play: you hear the other three and sing the missing one, or leave three out to hear the one you\'re learning.'],
                    ['Which chords can it voice?', 'Major, minor, diminished and augmented chords, sus2 and sus4, sixths, added ninths, and sevenths of every kind. Richer chords, like 9 or 13, are sung as the seventh chord under them, so four voices can sing them.'],
                    ['Is it free?', 'Yes: no account and no ads. Your chords and settings stay on this device.'],
                ],
                // What's worth knowing around the tool: the parts' ranges (the app's arranger keeps each part inside them).
                'guide' => [
                    [
                        'heading' => 'The four parts and their ranges',
                        'body' => [
                            'Each part\'s comfortable range, as most choir music keeps it. The generator keeps every part inside these, around the middle.',
                        ],
                        'table' => [
