            'mic-test' => [
                'icon' => 'mic',
                'eyebrow' => 'Level · 5-second take · fixes by browser',
                'name' => 'Mic test',
                'summary' => 'Check your mic works, at a good level, and hear it back.',
                'tie' => 'Your mic hears you. Now let it find your home note.',
                'tag' => 'Check your mic hears you',
                'group' => 'setup',
                'title' => 'Mic Test: Check Your Microphone, Hear It Back',
                'description' => 'Is your mic working? See its level, record 5 seconds and hear it back, and get this browser\'s fix if it\'s blocked. Free, no account, nothing sent.',
                'headline' => 'Mic test',
                'lead' => 'Check your microphone in a few seconds: is it allowed, is it hearing you, how loud, and how you sound. Nothing leaves this page.',
                'how' => [
                    'Tap Test my mic and allow the microphone when your browser asks. Talk or sing: the meter should reach the green, and the word under it says the level, like Good level or A little quiet.',
                    'Tap Record 5 s, talk or sing, then Play my take to hear yourself. If the mic is blocked, it shows the steps for your browser.',
                    'The mic\'s name shows under the meter; with more than one, pick another there. More has the details and turns the mic off.',
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['How do I know if my mic is working?', 'Tap Test my mic, allow it, and talk: if the meter moves and the word says Good level, it\'s working. To be sure, tap Record 5 s and play it back. If it says No sound after a few seconds, the mic is probably muted, or it\'s listening to a different one.'],
                    ['How do I allow the microphone in Chrome, Safari or Firefox?', 'From the address bar. In Chrome and Edge, click the icon left of the address; in Firefox, the crossed-out mic; in Safari on a Mac, the page menu in the address bar, then Website Settings (on older versions, the Safari menu); on an iPhone, the page menu, then Website Settings; on Android, the icon left of the address, then Permissions. If the site is allowed and it\'s still blocked, your system is blocking the browser itself. The table on this page has each browser and system, and the test shows the steps for yours.'],
                    ['Why is my mic so quiet?', 'Usually it\'s too far away, or its input volume is turned down. Come closer, then raise the input level: on a Mac in System Settings, Sound, Input; on Windows in Settings, System, Sound, under Input; on an audio interface, with its gain knob. This test doesn\'t boost a quiet mic the way call apps do, so it shows the level your mic really gives.'],
                    ['Why does my mic sound muffled or robotic?', 'Most often it\'s a call app\'s cleanup, a Bluetooth headset, or on a call a weak connection. Noise suppression and echo cancellation can smear a voice, and a Bluetooth headset\'s own mic switches to a low-quality call mode. This test records with that cleanup off: if your take sounds clear here but bad on calls, check the app\'s audio settings or use a wired mic.'],
                    ['Can I hear myself back?', 'Yes: Record 5 s keeps a five-second take, and Play my take plays it as often as you like. It doesn\'t play you live while you talk, because through speakers the mic would hear itself and howl, and in headphones you\'d hear yourself a little late.'],
                    ['Is my voice recorded or uploaded?', 'Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is recorded unless you tap Record 5 s. The mic opens only when you tap Test my mic. It stays in this page\'s memory, is replaced by your next take, and is gone when you close the page.'],
                    ['Why does my held note wobble on calls?', 'Because call apps are built for speech. Noise suppression can take a long, steady note for background noise and turn it down, and auto gain keeps pushing the level up and down, so a held note swells and fades. For singing, look for a music or original sound setting in your call app; this test hears you as Stackapella does, with echo cancellation, noise suppression and auto gain off.'],
                    ['Which microphone is it using, and how do I switch?', 'Its name shows under the meter once it\'s on. With more than one mic, the name becomes a menu: pick another and the test moves to it, and it\'s remembered on this device. Your call or game app picks its own mic, so change it in that app\'s audio settings too.'],
                    ['Why is another app blocking my mic?', 'If it says The mic is busy, another program is holding it, often a call or streaming app like Zoom, Teams, Discord or OBS. Close it or end its call, then tap Test my mic again. It happens most on Windows.'],
                    ['How loud should my voice be?', 'In the green part of the meter, with your loudest notes kept out of the amber bars at the top. There the word says Good level; in the amber it says Too loud, where a recording can distort. The levels in numbers are on this page, under What the level words mean.'],
                ],
                // Below the tool: what's worth knowing around it (docs/tools/seo-2.md), with one reference table:
                // the fix for a blocked mic, by browser and system (the test itself shows only this visitor's).
                'guide' => [
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Allow a blocked mic, by browser',
                        'body' => [
                            'There are two locks: the browser\'s permission for this site, and your system\'s permission for the browser. Open the first; if the mic is still blocked, check the second. When it\'s blocked, the test above shows the steps for the browser you\'re using, and once you allow it, it usually starts again by itself; if not, tap Test my mic.',
                        ],
                        'table' => [
                            'head' => ['Browser or system', 'Where to allow the microphone'],
                            'rows' => [
                                ['Chrome on a computer', 'Click the icon left of the address and turn Microphone on, or click the crossed-out mic at the right of the address bar and allow it. In Settings: Privacy and security, Site settings, Microphone.'],
                                ['Edge', 'Click the icon left of the address, then set Microphone to Allow. In Settings: Privacy, search, and services, Site permissions, Microphone (Cookies and site permissions in older versions).'],
                                ['Firefox on a computer', 'Click the crossed-out mic in the address bar and clear the block, then tap Test my mic again. In Settings: Privacy & Security, Permissions, Microphone.'],
                                ['Safari on a Mac', 'Click the page menu in the address bar, Website Settings, then Microphone: Allow (on older versions, the Safari menu, Settings for this website). For every site: Safari, Settings, Websites, Microphone.'],
                                ['Safari on iPhone and iPad', 'Tap the page menu in the address bar (aA on older versions), Website Settings, Microphone: Allow. For every site: Settings, Apps, Safari, Microphone (on iOS 17 and earlier, Settings, Safari).'],
                                ['Chrome or Firefox on iPhone', 'Settings, Privacy & Security, Microphone, and turn the browser on.'],
                                ['Chrome on Android', 'Tap the icon left of the address, then Permissions, Microphone. Still greyed out? See Android below.'],
                                ['Android', 'Settings, Apps, your browser, Permissions, Microphone. Also check that Microphone access is on in Quick Settings.'],
                                ['Mac', 'System Settings, Privacy & Security, Microphone, and turn your browser on. Safari isn\'t listed there: allow it in Safari itself.'],
                                ['Windows 11', 'Settings, Privacy & security, Microphone: turn on Microphone access, Let apps access your microphone, and Let desktop apps access your microphone.'],
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'What the level words mean',
                        'body' => [
                            'Good level: your voice is in the green, clear of the top. A little quiet: you\'re heard, but come a bit closer. Very quiet: only a faint signal, so come closer or turn the input up. Too loud: you\'re in the amber at the top, where a recording can distort, so back off a little. No sound: nothing for several seconds, so check for a mute switch or pick another mic.',
                            'In numbers, for those who want them: the meter reads to 0 dBFS, the green starts at -48 dBFS, Good level begins at about -40 dBFS and Too loud at -10 dBFS, all average (RMS) levels. Under More, Room says how much noise the mic hears between your phrases.',
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Testing a mic for singing',
                        'body' => [
                            'Call apps clean up a voice for speech, and that cleanup is hard on singing: a long note fades or wobbles, soft passages get cut, and the level pumps. This test, like Stackapella, records with echo cancellation, noise suppression and auto gain off, so your take is how your voice really reaches the mic.',
                            'Set the level on your loudest note, not your speaking voice, and leave room above it. Sing into your phone\'s or laptop\'s own mic, a wired headset or a USB mic rather than a Bluetooth headset\'s mic, which drops your headphones to call quality. To sing along to a track, wired headphones keep you in time, because Bluetooth adds a delay.',
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'What a browser test can\'t check',
                        'body' => [
                            'It checks the mic, your browser and your system. A call or game app picks its own input and has its own cleanup, so if the mic works here but not in the app, open that app\'s audio settings, choose the same mic, and check it isn\'t muted there.',
                            'It can\'t test your camera, and it hears only what reaches the mic. Playing your take back does check your speakers or headphones.',
                        ],
                    ],
                ],
