                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'The three chords, tone by tone',
                        'body' => [
                            'Each tone is measured from the root, which the drone holds at the piano\'s pitch, so the root is the zero.',
                            'Major: the third sits 13.7 cents below the piano and the fifth 2.0 above. Minor: the third sits 15.6 above and the fifth 2.0 above. Barbershop seventh: the third 13.7 below, the fifth 2.0 above and the seventh 31.2 below.',
                            'So in a major chord the third comes down, in a minor chord it goes up, and in a barbershop seventh the seventh comes down most of all. The tiles under the meter show the same numbers.',
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Hearing it: beats',
                        'body' => [
                            'Two notes a little out of tune beat: a wobble in the sound, faster the further apart they are. A piano\'s major third on middle C beats about ten times a second; sung pure, the beats stop.',
                            'So listen as well as look. As you move toward pure, the wobble against the drone slows, and when it stops the needle sits in the green. Tap a tile first to hear where the tone belongs. It works the same for any instrument that can bend its pitch, like a violin, a trombone or a clarinet, played in a voice\'s range.',
                        ],
