                                ['Minor 7th', '0, 3, 7, 10', 'C, E♭, G, B♭', 'Mellow, relaxed'],
                                ['Half-diminished 7th', '0, 3, 6, 10', 'C, E♭, G♭, B♭', 'Dark, searching'],
                                ['Diminished 7th', '0, 3, 6, 9', 'C, E♭, G♭, B double flat (sounds as A)', 'Very tense, suspenseful'],
                            ],
                            'links' => ['/chords/major', '/chords/minor', '/chords/diminished', '/chords/augmented', '/chords/dominant-seventh', '/chords/major-seventh', '/chords/minor-seventh', '/chords/half-diminished', '/chords/diminished-seventh'],
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Major or minor: the one note that changes',
                        'body' => [
                            'Sing a major chord from the bottom, root, third, fifth, then lower the middle note by a semitone and sing it again: that\'s minor. Nothing else moves. So when you\'re stuck on major or minor, listen for that middle note.',
                            'The other triads change one more note. Diminished is minor with the fifth lowered too; augmented is major with the fifth raised. The sevenths add a fourth note on top of one of these.',
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Inversions and voicings, sung',
                        'body' => [
                            'Here each chord is sung close together, a voice to a note, with the root at the bottom unless you turn on Inversions too. Then any note can be the lowest, and a seventh chord can even have its seventh at the bottom.',
                            'In a choir the same chord is often spread wider, with the bass well below the others and a note doubled. It\'s still the same quality, and once your ear knows the close shape, it starts to find it in the wide one too.',
