                    [
                        'heading' => 'Tips for tuning a ukulele',
                        'body' => [
                            'Tune up to the note: if a string is sharp, go a little below and come back up, so it holds. Pluck gently, near the middle of the string; a hard pluck starts sharp. New strings stretch for days, so a new ukulele drifts flat quickly: tune it, play a while, and tune again.',
                        ],
                    ],
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['What are the notes of a ukulele?', 'In standard tuning, G, C, E and A, from the string nearest your chin to the one nearest the floor. The G is high, above middle C, which is called re-entrant tuning.'],
                    ['How do I tune a ukulele without a tuner?', 'Tune one string to a reference, then the rest to it. Tap the A here and tune your A string until there is no wobble between them; then the E string at the 5th fret should match the open A, the C string at the 4th fret the open E, and the G string at the 2nd fret the open A.'],
                    ['Does it work for soprano, concert and tenor ukuleles?', 'Yes: all three use the same G C E A tuning. A baritone ukulele is tuned D G B E, like a guitar\'s top four strings, so pick Guitar in Settings and tune those four.'],
                    ['What about low G tuning?', 'Low G tunes the G string an octave down, to G3 (196.00 Hz), for a fuller sound. The string targets here are high G, so pick Chromatic in Settings and tune that string to G3.'],
                    ['Why does my ukulele keep going out of tune?', 'New strings stretch a lot for their first week or so; keep retuning and they settle. Changes in temperature and humidity move the tuning too.'],
                    ['Is my playing recorded?', 'No. The microphone opens only when you tap Listen, the pitch is worked out on this device, and nothing is recorded, kept or sent.'],
                ],
