                'related' => ['Tuning an instrument? The tuner does those too', 'tuner'],
                'eyebrow' => 'For singing · calm on vibrato · your pitch over time',
                'title' => 'Voice Tuner: See the Note You\'re Singing',
                'description' => 'A free online voice tuner: sing a note and see which one it is, how many cents sharp or flat, and your pitch over time. Calm on vibrato. No account.',
                'headline' => 'Voice tuner',
                'lead' => 'Sing one note and see which note it is, how many cents sharp or flat, and a line of your pitch over time, so you see where it drifts. It smooths out vibrato, so a held note reads steadily.',
                'how' => [
                    'Tap Listen and allow the microphone. Sing an easy "ah" on one note and hold it for a few seconds.',
                    'Watch the line as well as the needle: it shows a note that sags at the end of a breath, or starts under the note and slides up. In Settings, set how close counts as In tune.',
                ],
                'guide' => [
                    [
                        'heading' => 'What a voice tuner shows',
                        'body' => [
                            'The big letter is the note you\'re on and the small number its octave: middle C is C4. The cents say how far you are from that note, minus for flat and plus for sharp; a cent is a hundredth of a semitone. The line draws your pitch over the last few seconds.',
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Practising with a voice tuner',
                        'body' => [
                            'Sing the note first, decide whether you think you\'re flat or sharp, and only then look. That way the tuner trains your ear instead of standing in for it. Start on comfortable notes in the middle of your voice, and use it for single notes: a choir singing a chord in tune leans toward pure intervals, which a tuner reads as slightly off.',
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'A voice tuner is not autotune',
                        'body' => [
                            'This shows your pitch so you can learn to correct it yourself. It doesn\'t change or record your voice. Autotune is a different kind of tool, one that changes the pitch of a voice, live or in a recording.',
                        ],
                    ],
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['What is a voice tuner?', 'A tuner made for singing: it names the note you sing, shows how far sharp or flat you are, and smooths out vibrato so a held note reads steadily.'],
                    ['Is this autotune?', 'No. It shows your pitch so you can hear and correct it yourself. Nothing changes your voice, and nothing is recorded.'],
                    ['Why does the needle wobble when I sing?', 'Vibrato and the small natural wavers of a voice. Voice mode smooths them out; a steady line around the note is what to aim for.'],
                    ['How close counts as in tune?', 'Within 10 cents, held for half a second, in Voice mode. You can set that from 2 to 15 cents in Settings, tighter as you improve.'],
                    ['Can it tell me my vocal range?', 'Not this one: try the vocal range test, which finds your lowest and highest notes and the voice type that fits.'],
                    ['Is my voice recorded?', 'No. The microphone opens only when you tap Listen, your pitch is worked out on this device, and nothing is recorded, kept or sent.'],
                ],
            ],
            'tanpura' => [
                'name' => 'Tanpura',
                'summary' => 'Pa, Sa, Sa and low Sa, plucked in turn in any key.',
                'tag' => 'Any Sa, Pa, Ma or Ni',
                'related' => ['Want a steady drone or a pure chord? Try drone tones', 'drone'],
                'eyebrow' => 'Any key · Pa, Ma or Ni · pure or equal',
                'title' => 'Online Tanpura: Free Drone for Singing',
                'description' => 'A free online tanpura: Pa, Sa, Sa and low Sa plucked in turn in any key, pure or equal, slow or quick. For riyaz and singing in tune. No account.',
                'headline' => 'Online tanpura',
                'lead' => 'Pick your Sa and press Play: four strings plucked in turn round your key note, Pa, Sa, Sa and low Sa, for as long as you practise. The first string can be Ma or Ni instead.',
                'how' => [
                    'Pick the key of your Sa at the top, then press Play. The strings keep plucking while you sing.',
                    'Choose the first string, Pa, Ma, Ni or none, to suit the raag. Under More, set the pace (Slow, Steady or Quick) and the reference A.',
                ],
                'guide' => [
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Choosing your Sa',
                        'body' => [
                            'Sa is your home note: a relaxed note low in the middle of your voice, so both the lower notes and the upper Sa are easy. Many men sing with Sa around C to D, many women around G to A sharp. The key control at the top moves it a half step at a time.',
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Pa, Ma and Ni',
                        'body' => [
                            'The first string is usually Pa, the fifth. For a raag without Pa, such as Malkauns, tune it to Ma, the fourth; some, such as Marwa, use Ni. None leaves it out.',
                        ],
                    ],
                    [
                        'heading' => 'Pure or equal',
                        'body' => [
                            'A tanpura is tuned by ear, so its Pa is a pure fifth, 2 cents wider than a keyboard\'s. Pure does the same; Equal matches a harmonium or keyboard.',
                        ],
                    ],
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['What is a tanpura?', 'The drone of Indian classical music: a long-necked instrument whose strings, usually four, are plucked in turn round the singer\'s key note, Sa. It plays no melody; it holds the home note so every other note can be measured against it.'],
                    ['How do you practise with a tanpura?', 'Sing Sa and hold it until it settles into the drone, then Pa, then slow scales, stopping on each note. This daily practice is often called riyaz.'],
                    ['Which key should my Sa be in?', 'Wherever a relaxed, low-middle note sits in your voice. Set Sa there, then move it a half step at a time until the lower notes and the upper Sa both feel easy.'],
                    ['Why does it sound different from a real tanpura?', 'A real tanpura\'s curved bridge, the jawari, gives each string its buzzing, shimmering overtones. This one is synthesized: close enough to sing to, not a recording.'],
                    ['Is a shruti box the same thing?', 'Close. A shruti box holds its notes steadily with bellows, where a tanpura plucks them in turn. Drone, the other mode here, holds notes like a shruti box.'],
                    ['Can it keep playing while I lock the screen?', 'It keeps playing in another tab, and the phone\'s media controls can pause it. On an iPhone, Safari stops a web page\'s sound when the screen locks, so while it plays it keeps your screen on (switch that off under More).'],
                ],
            ],
