                    'formula' => '1 3 ♯5',
                    'in_c' => 'C E G♯',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Three notes · 1 3 ♯5 · pure 16:20:25',
                    'title' => 'Augmented Chord: Notes, Sound and Tuning',
                    'description' => 'An augmented chord is two major thirds: C, E and G♯ in C. See why it sounds unresolved and where it moves, and hear it played and tuned pure.',
                    'headline' => 'The augmented chord',
                    'lead' => 'An augmented chord is two major thirds stacked: a root, a major third, and an augmented fifth 8 semitones up. It sounds stretched and unresolved, as if it\'s waiting for the next chord.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'How it\'s built',
                            'body' => [
                                'It\'s a major chord with the fifth raised a semitone: C, E and G♯. On a piano every gap in it is a major third, including the one from G♯ up to the next C, so it splits the octave into three equal parts. That makes it symmetrical: C, E and G♯ augmented use the same three keys, and a piano has only four different augmented chords.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing it in tune',
                            'body' => [
                                'Switched to Pure, this page tunes it as two pure major thirds, 16:20:25. E sits 13.7 cents (a cent is a hundredth of a semitone) below the piano\'s, and G♯, a pure third above E, 27.4 cents below. Three pure major thirds fall 41 cents short of an octave, so the gap from G♯ up to the next C comes out wide: 427.4 cents against the piano\'s 400.',
                                'This is where pure and equal tuning disagree most. G♯ tuned as a third above E sits 41 cents lower than A♭ tuned pure as a minor sixth over C, though a piano plays both on one key. In practice the raised fifth is usually a moving note, rising a semitone into the next chord, so the line it\'s heading for matters as much as the chord.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Where you hear it',
                            'body' => [
                                'Most often between two chords, as one voice climbs by semitones: C, C augmented, then F or A minor, with G rising to G♯ and on to A. It\'s also the chord on the third note of the harmonic minor scale, E♭, G and B in C minor.',
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['What notes are in a C augmented chord?', 'C, E and G♯. It\'s written G♯ rather than A♭ because it\'s the fifth of C, raised a semitone.'],
                        ['What does the + symbol mean?', 'Augmented: C+ and Caug are the same chord. In a chord symbol, + on its own means the fifth is raised.'],
                        ['What is the difference between augmented and diminished chords?', 'An augmented chord stacks two major thirds and raises the fifth; a diminished chord stacks two minor thirds and lowers it. Both are tense, but augmented sounds stretched and diminished sounds squeezed.'],
                        ['Why does an augmented chord sound unresolved?', 'Because it\'s symmetrical: its notes split the octave into three equal major thirds, which gives no note the clear feel of a root, so the ear can\'t tell where it\'s going. It usually resolves by one note moving a semitone.'],
                    ],
