                    'title' => 'Major 7th Chord: Notes, Sound and Tuning',
                    'description' => 'A major 7th chord is a major chord plus a major seventh: Cmaj7 is C, E, G and B. See how it differs from a dominant 7th, and hear it played and tuned pure.',
                    'headline' => 'The major 7th chord',
                    'lead' => 'A major 7th chord is a major chord with a major seventh on top, a semitone below the octave. It sounds soft, warm and a little dreamy: more colour than tension.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'How it\'s built',
                            'body' => [
                                'Stack a major third, a minor third and another major third: C, E, G and B. The seventh is 11 semitones above the root. Hidden inside are two chords, C major at the bottom and E minor at the top.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing it in tune',
                            'body' => [
                                'Tuned pure, it\'s 8:10:12:15. The third sits 13.7 cents (a cent is a hundredth of a semitone) below the piano\'s, the fifth 2.0 above, and the seventh, 15:8, 11.7 below. The seventh is a pure major third above the fifth and a pure fifth above the third, which is why it can sit so calmly in the chord.',
                                'In a melody, many singers push a leading note (the seventh note of the scale) up toward the root. Held in a major 7th chord it wants the opposite, a touch lower than the piano, which makes the semitone up to the root a wide 16:15, 111.7 cents.',
                            ],
