                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['What is the difference between a major 7th and a dominant 7th chord?', 'The seventh. A major 7th chord has the major seventh, 11 semitones up (B over C), and a dominant 7th has the minor seventh, 10 up (B♭). The major 7th can rest; the dominant 7th wants to move.'],
                        ['Why is it written maj7 and not just 7?', 'Because a plain 7 means the dominant 7th: C7 is C, E, G and B♭. Cmaj7, CM7 and CΔ7 all mean C, E, G and B.'],
                        ['How should a choir tune a major 7th chord?', 'With the seventh and the third both a touch low: the pure seventh, 15:8, is 11.7 cents below the piano\'s, and the pure third 13.7 cents below. Tune the root and fifth first, then the third, then the seventh against them.'],
