                'dominant-seventh' => [
                    'name' => 'Dominant 7th chord',
                    'summary' => 'A major chord with a minor seventh: it pulls home, and tuned pure, it rings.',
                    'symbol' => '7',
                    'formula' => '1 3 5 ♭7',
                    'in_c' => 'C E G B♭',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Four notes · 1 3 5 ♭7 · pure 4:5:6:7',
                    'title' => 'Dominant 7th Chord and the Barbershop Seventh',
                    'description' => 'A dominant 7th is a major chord plus a minor seventh: C7 is C, E, G and B♭. See how it resolves, and why barbershop singers tune its seventh low. Hear it.',
                    'headline' => 'The dominant 7th chord',
                    'lead' => 'A dominant 7th chord is a major chord with a minor seventh on top. It sounds strong but unfinished and pulls toward the chord a fifth below; tuned pure, it\'s the ringing barbershop seventh.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'How it\'s built',
                            'body' => [
                                'Stack a major third and two minor thirds: C, E, G and B♭. The name comes from where it usually sits, on the dominant, the fifth note of the key: in C major that\'s G7, which is G, B, D and F. Between its third and its seventh, B and F, is a tritone, and that tritone is what pulls.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing it in tune',
                            'body' => [
                                'Tuned pure, it\'s 4:5:6:7, harmonics 4 to 7 (the overtones inside one note) of a note two octaves below the root. The third sits 13.7 cents (a cent is a hundredth of a semitone) below the piano\'s, the fifth 2.0 above, and the seventh, 7:4, 31.2 below: nearly a third of a semitone. That seventh is far enough from the piano\'s to sound wrong on its own, until all four notes lock and the chord rings.',
                                'The tritone between the third and the seventh tunes to 7:5, 17.5 cents narrower than the piano\'s. Barbershop singers build their sound on this chord, and many talk of a fifth voice: when it locks, the reinforced overtones can sound like a note no one is singing.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Where you hear it',
                            'body' => [
                                'Its classic move is V7 to I, G7 to C. The third, B, rises to C, and the seventh, F, falls to E, so the tritone closes in. In a jazz ii-V-I it\'s the V: Dm7, G7, Cmaj7.',
                                'Barbershop arrangements are full of it, often in chains round the circle of fifths: E7, A7, D7, G7, then C. The blues puts it on every chord, C7, F7 and G7, with no need to resolve.',
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['Why is it called a dominant 7th?', 'Because it\'s the seventh chord built on the dominant, the fifth note of a major or harmonic minor scale. In C that\'s G7, and the same shape on any root keeps the name.'],
                        ['What is the difference between a dominant 7th and a major 7th chord?', 'The seventh. A dominant 7th has the minor seventh, 10 semitones up (B♭ over C), and a major 7th has the major seventh, 11 up (B). That one semitone changes a chord that pulls into one that rests.'],
                        ['What is a barbershop seventh?', 'A dominant 7th tuned pure, 4:5:6:7, with its seventh 31.2 cents below the piano\'s. The low seventh lines its overtones up with the other notes\', so the chord stops beating and rings.'],
                        ['Why does a dominant 7th want to resolve?', 'Because of the tritone between its third and seventh. In G7 that\'s B and F, and the strongest way to settle it is B up to C and F down to E, which lands on the C major chord.'],
                    ],
                ],
                'diminished-seventh' => [
                    'name' => 'Diminished 7th chord',
                    'summary' => 'Three minor thirds: tense, suspenseful, and able to lead almost anywhere.',
                    'symbol' => 'dim7',
                    'tones' => [9 => 'Diminished seventh'],
                    'formula' => '1 ♭3 ♭5 𝄫7',
                    'in_c' => 'C E♭ G♭ B𝄫',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Four notes · 1 ♭3 ♭5 𝄫7 · pure 15:18:21:25',
                    'title' => 'Diminished 7th Chord: Notes, Sound, Tuning',
                    'description' => 'A diminished 7th chord is three minor thirds: C, E♭, G♭ and B𝄫 in C. See why a piano has only three of them, and hear it played and tuned pure.',
                    'headline' => 'The diminished 7th chord',
                    'lead' => 'A diminished 7th chord is three minor thirds stacked, splitting the octave into four equal parts on a piano. It sounds tense and suspenseful, and it can lead almost anywhere.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'How it\'s built',
                            'body' => [
                                'On C it\'s C, E♭, G♭ and B𝄫. The double flat is a seventh lowered twice, and it sounds as A; spelled as a stack of thirds it needs the double flat, though many chord charts simply write A.',
                                'On a piano every gap in it is a minor third, including the one from the top note up to the next C. So C, E♭, G♭ and A diminished 7th use the same four keys, and a piano has only three different diminished 7th chords.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing it in tune',
                            'body' => [
                                'Pure tuning breaks the symmetry, because it\'s measured from the root. Switched to Pure, this page tunes it 15:18:21:25: the minor third 15.6 cents (a cent is a hundredth of a semitone) above the piano\'s, the diminished fifth, 7:5, 17.5 below, and the diminished seventh, 5:3, 15.6 below. Its four thirds come out unequal, from 266.9 cents between the third and the fifth to 315.6 at the bottom and the top.',
                                'So there\'s no single pure version that suits every root. Move the root with − and + and the same four piano keys tune differently, depending on which one is treated as the root.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Where you hear it',
                            'body' => [
                                'In a minor key it\'s the chord on the seventh note: B, D, F and A♭ in C minor, pulling to C minor. Any of its notes can act as the leading note (the note just below the home note) of a different key, which made it a favourite for changing key in Romantic music, and for suspense in melodrama and silent film. In barbershop and close harmony it often appears as a passing chord.',
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['Why does a diminished 7th chord have a double flat?', 'Because its seventh is diminished: a minor seventh lowered one more semitone. On C, the major seventh is B, the minor seventh B♭, and the diminished seventh B𝄫, which sounds as A.'],
                        ['Why are there only three diminished 7th chords?', 'Because on a piano it splits the octave into four equal minor thirds, so each chord shares its notes with three others. Starting on C, C♯ and D covers them all; on D♯ you\'re back to C\'s notes.'],
                        ['What is the difference between diminished 7th and half-diminished?', 'The seventh. A diminished 7th has a diminished seventh, 9 semitones up (B𝄫 over C), and a half-diminished has a minor seventh, 10 up (B♭). The diminished 7th is tenser and more symmetrical.'],
                        ['Is dim the same as dim7?', 'No: Cdim is the three-note diminished chord, C, E♭ and G♭, and Cdim7 adds the diminished seventh. Some chord charts write dim when they mean dim7, so check the arrangement.'],
                    ],
                ],
                'half-diminished' => [
                    'name' => 'Half-diminished chord',
                    'summary' => 'A diminished chord with a minor seventh: dark and searching, also m7♭5.',
                    'symbol' => 'm7♭5',
                    'formula' => '1 ♭3 ♭5 ♭7',
                    'in_c' => 'C E♭ G♭ B♭',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Four notes · 1 ♭3 ♭5 ♭7 · pure 5:6:7:9',
                    'title' => 'Half-Diminished Chord: m7♭5 Notes and Sound',
                    'description' => 'A half-diminished chord (m7♭5) is a diminished chord plus a minor seventh: C, E♭, G♭ and B♭. See how it leads in a minor ii-V-i, and hear it played.',
                    'headline' => 'The half-diminished chord',
                    'lead' => 'A half-diminished chord is a diminished chord with a minor seventh on top, also called minor 7 flat 5. It sounds dark and searching, softer than the full diminished 7th.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'How it\'s built',
                            'body' => [
                                'Stack two minor thirds and a major third: C, E♭, G♭ and B♭. It\'s half diminished because only the triad is diminished; the seventh is a minor seventh, not a diminished one. Its top three notes make an E♭ minor chord, so Cm7♭5 has the same notes as E♭m6.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing it in tune',
                            'body' => [
                                'Switched to Pure, this page tunes it 5:6:7:9. The minor third sits 15.6 cents (a cent is a hundredth of a semitone) above the piano\'s, the diminished fifth, 7:5, 17.5 below, and the seventh, 9:5, 17.6 above. Between the ♭5 and the seventh is 9:7, a wide major third, 35.1 cents wider than the piano\'s.',
                                'Those are harmonics 5, 6, 7 and 9: a dominant 9th chord, 4:5:6:7:9, with its root left out. B, D, F and A, Bm7♭5, are G9 without the G, which is why the chord so often does a dominant\'s job.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Where you hear it',
                            'body' => [
                                'On the second note of a minor key, leading to the dominant: Dm7♭5, G7, C minor, the minor ii-V-i of jazz. In a major key it\'s the chord on the seventh note, Bm7♭5 in C. The opening of Wagner\'s Tristan und Isolde, from 1859, famously centres on a chord with this sound, F, B, D♯ and G♯, known as the Tristan chord.',
                            ],
                        ],
