            'pages' => [
                'bass' => [
                    'name' => 'Bass',
                    'summary' => 'The lowest voice, deep and dark, the foundation of every chord.',
                    'range' => 'E2 to E4',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Usual range E2 to E4 · sings Bass',
                    'title' => 'Bass Vocal Range: Notes, Part and Tips',
                    'description' => 'The bass voice\'s usual range is E2 to E4. Hear its notes, see how to tell a bass from a baritone, and learn what the bass part does in a choir.',
                    'headline' => 'The bass voice',
                    'lead' => 'The bass is the lowest of the usual voices, with a usual range from E2 to E4. It sounds deep and dark, and it sings the Bass part that holds up a choir\'s chords.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'The bass range',
                            'body' => [
                                'A bass\'s usual range runs from E2 to E4, two octaves. E2 is the E an octave and a sixth below middle C, the same note as a guitar\'s lowest string. E4 is the E just above middle C, the same as the guitar\'s highest string. Most basses are at home in the lower middle of that, from about A2 to B3.',
                                'Some trained basses go lower, to D2 or even C2, and many have a few notes above E4. The bottom notes come and go with the day: many singers find a note or two more in the morning, and fewer when they are tired or tense.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Am I a bass or a baritone?',
                            'body' => [
                                'The two ranges are only a few notes apart, so listen to the tone, not just the notes. A bass keeps a full, solid sound down to about F2 or E2. A baritone can often reach those notes, but they come out thin, breathy or very quiet.',
                                'Then listen to the top. Many basses feel the voice change gear around middle C, and the notes above it soon feel like hard work; many baritones go on comfortably to D4 or E4. Colour helps too: a bass sounds darker and heavier. If you sit between, you may be a bass-baritone, and you can sing Bass in most choirs.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing bass in a choir',
                            'body' => [
                                'The bass part is the bottom of the chord. It often sings the root, the note the chord is named after, so the other parts tune to it, and a steady bass line keeps the whole choir in tune and in time.',
                                'Choir bass parts mostly sit from about G2 to C4 and seldom go below F2 or E2, so a huge low range isn\'t needed. Most of the part is in chest voice: sing the low notes relaxed, without pressing down, because a light, clear low note tunes better than a forced one. Sing the high notes, near D4, a little lighter.',
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['What is the lowest note a bass can sing?', 'The usual bass range bottoms out at E2, the note of a guitar\'s lowest string. Some trained basses reach D2, a few C2, and a rare few go lower still, but choir bass parts seldom ask for anything below E2.'],
                        ['How do I know if I\'m a bass?', 'Start with your lowest notes: if F2 or E2 comes out with a full tone, and your voice changes gear around middle C, bass is a good guess. The vocal range test finds your lowest and highest notes; then try the bass part of a song and see if it feels like home.'],
                        ['Can I make my voice lower?', 'A little, and mostly by making the low notes you have clearer and steadier. Relaxed, regular practice helps, but it won\'t change what kind of voice you have, and pushing down hard can hurt. Sing low notes gently, and stop if it strains; a teacher can help.'],
                        ['Is bass the lowest voice type?', 'Yes, bass is the lowest of the six usual voice types. Classical singing has names for especially low basses, such as basso profondo, but in a choir the Bass part is the bottom line.'],
                    ],
                ],
                'baritone' => [
                    'name' => 'Baritone',
                    'summary' => 'The middle men\'s voice, warm and full, between bass and tenor.',
                    'range' => 'G2 to G4',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Usual range G2 to G4 · sings Bass or Tenor 2',
                    'title' => 'What Is a Baritone? Baritone Range and Part',
                    'description' => 'A baritone is the middle men\'s voice, between bass and tenor, usually G2 to G4. Hear its notes, tell it from a tenor or a bass, and find your choir part.',
                    'headline' => 'The baritone voice',
                    'lead' => 'A baritone is the middle men\'s voice, between bass and tenor, with a usual range from G2 to G4. It sounds warm and full through the middle, and in a choir it sings Bass or Tenor 2.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'The baritone range',
                            'body' => [
                                'A baritone\'s usual range runs from G2, the G an octave and a fourth below middle C, to G4, the G just above middle C: two octaves. Most baritones are at home in the middle of it, from about C3 to D4.',
                                'Trained baritones often reach A4 or higher, and many have a note or two below G2, though they get quieter down there. Baritone is often called the most common men\'s voice, and plenty of men sit between baritone and one of its neighbours.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Am I a baritone, a tenor or a bass?',
                            'body' => [
                                'Against a tenor, look at the top. Many baritones feel the voice change gear around D4 or E4, and a song that sits around E4 to G4 tires them. A tenor changes later, around F4 or G4, and can stay up there. Against a bass, look at the bottom: a bass keeps a full, solid tone down to F2 or E2, where a baritone\'s low notes thin out. A bass also tends to change gear lower, around middle C.',
                                'Colour helps too: a baritone sounds lighter than a bass and warmer and fuller than a tenor in the middle. Voices in between are common: a bass-baritone sits between bass and baritone, and a high baritone often sings Tenor 2 well.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing baritone in a choir',
                            'body' => [
                                'Most mixed choirs have no baritone part, so a baritone sings Bass or Tenor 2. Bass suits a baritone when the part\'s lowest notes stay around G2 or above. Tenor 2, the lower line when the tenors split, suits a high baritone whose top is easy to about F4 or G4. The director chooses by the music and where voices are needed.',
                                'In music for men\'s voices, the part may be called Baritone. In barbershop, the baritone sings a harmony note that crosses above and below the lead, who has the tune. Wherever you sing, keep the top notes light rather than pushing chest voice up, and stop if it strains.',
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['What is the baritone vocal range?', 'The usual baritone range is G2 to G4: two octaves, from the G an octave and a fourth below middle C to the G just above it. Trained baritones often go beyond it at the top, and every voice is a little different.'],
                        ['Should a baritone sing bass or tenor in a choir?', 'Either can work: a baritone usually sings Bass or Tenor 2. If the tenor part\'s top notes feel easy, Tenor 2 suits you; if the bass part\'s lowest notes still have tone, Bass does. The director decides by the music and where voices are needed.'],
                        ['Can a baritone sing tenor songs?', 'Often yes, in a lower key. Moving a song down a few semitones keeps the tune the same and brings its high notes into a baritone\'s easy range; on Stackapella you can move a song up or down until it sits right. What to avoid is pushing for notes above your easy range.'],
                        ['Is baritone the most common male voice?', 'It is often called that, and many men\'s voices do sit in the baritone range. No count settles it, though, and plenty of men sit between baritone and tenor, or baritone and bass.'],
                    ],
                ],
                'tenor' => [
