                    'summary' => 'The higher men\'s voice, bright and ringing at the top.',
                    'range' => 'C3 to A4',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Usual range C3 to A4 · sings Tenor',
                    'title' => 'Tenor Vocal Range: Notes, Part and Tips',
                    'description' => 'The tenor\'s usual range is C3 to A4. Hear its notes, see how to tell a tenor from a baritone, and learn what the tenor part does in a choir.',
                    'headline' => 'The tenor voice',
                    'lead' => 'The tenor is the highest of the usual men\'s voices, with a usual range from C3 to A4. It sounds bright and ringing near the top, and it sings the Tenor part in a choir.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'The tenor range',
                            'body' => [
                                'A tenor\'s usual range runs from C3, the C an octave below middle C, to A4, the A just above middle C: the 440 Hz note an orchestra tunes to. That is a little under two octaves. A tenor is often most at home in the upper part of it, around middle C and above, where lower men\'s voices start to work hard.',
                                'These are usual notes, not limits. Trained tenors often sing B♭4 and C5, the note singers call a tenor\'s high C, and some go higher in head voice or falsetto. Below C3 most tenors still have a few notes, but they lose colour and volume.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Am I a tenor or a baritone?',
                            'body' => [
                                'The two ranges share everything from C3 to G4, so your top note alone won\'t tell you. Three things help more.',
                                'Where it\'s easy: sing a song that stays around E4 to G4. A tenor can stay there comfortably; a baritone usually tires. Where it changes gear: slide slowly up a scale on "ah". Many baritones feel the voice shift around D4 or E4, many tenors around F4 or G4. Colour: a tenor sounds brighter and lighter, a baritone warmer and fuller in the middle. If you sit between, you may be a high baritone or a low tenor: try both parts and choose the one you could sing all evening.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing tenor in a choir',
                            'body' => [
                                'The tenor part sits above the bass and below the alto, in the middle of the chord, and now and then takes the tune. On its own staff it\'s usually written in a treble clef with a small 8 below it, which means it sounds an octave lower than written; in hymn books it shares the bass clef with the basses.',
                                'Choir tenor parts mostly sit from about D3 to G4 and seldom go above A4, but they spend a lot of time near the top, around E4 to G4, right where the voice changes gear. Many tenors sing those notes in a lighter mix rather than full chest voice: it carries well and blends better. If a high note only comes by pushing, sing it lighter, and stop if it strains.',
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['What is the highest note a tenor can sing?', 'The usual tenor range tops out at A4, the A just above middle C. Trained tenors often reach C5, called a tenor\'s high C, and some go higher in head voice or falsetto. Choir tenor parts seldom go above A4.'],
                        ['Can a woman sing tenor?', 'Yes, and many choirs have women in the tenor section. They are usually altos with a strong, easy low range. They sing the same notes as the men, and the lowest ones, around C3 to E3, are usually the hardest part.'],
                        ['Is tenor higher than baritone?', 'Yes: the tenor is the higher of the two, with a usual range of C3 to A4 against the baritone\'s G2 to G4. The ranges share most of their notes, so the difference is more about where each voice is comfortable and where it changes gear.'],
                        ['What is a countertenor?', 'A countertenor is a man who sings in the alto range or higher, mostly in head voice or falsetto. It is a classical voice type of its own, not a kind of tenor, and in a choir a countertenor usually sings alto.'],
                    ],
                ],
                'alto' => [
                    'name' => 'Alto',
                    'summary' => 'The lower women\'s voice, rich and warm, singing the harmony below the tune.',
                    'range' => 'F3 to D5',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Usual range F3 to D5 · sings Alto',
                    'title' => 'What Is an Alto? Alto Vocal Range and Part',
                    'description' => 'An alto is the lowest usual women\'s voice, F3 to D5, singing the harmony just below the tune. Hear its notes and tell an alto from a mezzo or a soprano.',
                    'headline' => 'The alto voice',
                    'lead' => 'An alto is the lowest of the usual women\'s voices, singing the harmony just below the tune, with a usual range from F3 to D5. It sounds rich and warm low down, and in a choir it sings the Alto part.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'The alto range',
                            'body' => [
                                'An alto\'s usual range runs from F3, the F below middle C, to D5, the D just over an octave above middle C. Most altos are at home from about A3 to A4, with a strong, full sound around and below middle C, where higher voices lose power.',
                                'In classical singing, the lowest women\'s voice is called contralto, and alto is the name of the choir part. Everyday speech mixes the two, and an alto in a choir may be a contralto or a mezzo-soprano. Trained altos often sing a few notes beyond either end of this range.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Am I an alto or a mezzo-soprano?',
                            'body' => [
                                'The two ranges share everything from A3 to D5, so look at comfort and colour. An alto\'s low notes, around F3 to A3, come out full and easy; a mezzo can usually reach them, but with less weight. At the top, an alto starts to tire around C5 or D5, where a mezzo often still feels free up to E5 or F5.',
                                'Most women\'s voices change gear from chest voice to a lighter head voice somewhere a few notes above middle C, and altos often keep a strong chest voice a little higher. Colour is the last clue: an alto sounds darker and richer. If you sit between, Alto 1 in a split choir may suit you, and the vocal range test gives a first guess.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing alto in a choir',
                            'body' => [
                                'The alto part sits just below the soprano tune and fills in the middle of the chord. It often moves in small steps and repeats notes, and choir alto parts mostly sit from about G3 to C5. Sing the lower notes in a relaxed chest voice and lighten as you go up, so the change of gear above middle C stays smooth.',
                                'Holding a harmony while the tune sounds just above you is the alto\'s real skill, and it takes practice. Learn the part on its own first, then sing it against the tune. On Stackapella you can sing your part while hearing the others, then record it and hear how it fits.',
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['Am I an alto or a soprano?', 'Look at where your voice is happiest, not only at your top note. If about F3 to D5 feels at home for a whole song and the soprano\'s high notes, up toward A5, tire you, alto is the likely fit. If you can sing high and light all evening, try soprano; a mezzo-soprano sits between the two and often sings Alto 1.'],
                        ['What is the difference between alto and contralto?', 'Contralto is the classical name for the lowest women\'s voice type, and alto is the choir part, sung by contraltos and many mezzo-sopranos. In everyday speech the two words are often used for the same voice.'],
                        ['Can a man sing alto?', 'Yes. A man singing alto, called a countertenor or male alto, sings mostly in head voice or falsetto, and many church and cathedral choirs have them in the alto section.'],
                        ['What is the highest note an alto can sing?', 'The usual alto range tops out at D5, the D just over an octave above middle C. Many altos can sing higher, especially in head voice, but choir alto parts seldom go above C5 or D5.'],
                    ],
                ],
                'mezzo-soprano' => [
                    'name' => 'Mezzo-soprano',
                    'summary' => 'The middle women\'s voice, warm and full, between alto and soprano.',
                    'range' => 'A3 to F5',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Usual range A3 to F5 · sings Soprano 2 or Alto 1',
                    'title' => 'Mezzo-Soprano Vocal Range: Notes and Part',
                    'description' => 'The mezzo-soprano\'s usual range is A3 to F5. Hear its notes, see how to tell a mezzo from a soprano or an alto, and find the choir part to sing.',
                    'headline' => 'The mezzo-soprano voice',
                    'lead' => 'The mezzo-soprano is the middle women\'s voice, between alto and soprano, with a usual range from A3 to F5. It sounds warm and full through the middle, and in a choir it sings Soprano 2 or Alto 1.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'The mezzo-soprano range',
                            'body' => [
                                'A mezzo\'s usual range runs from A3, the A below middle C, to F5, the F an octave and a fourth above middle C. Mezzo is Italian for "middle" or "half": the voice sits between alto and soprano and shares most of its notes with both.',
                                'Many mezzos are most at home from about C4 to D5, with a rich lower middle. Trained mezzos often sing up to A5 or beyond, and many have a few notes below A3.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Am I a mezzo-soprano, a soprano or an alto?',
                            'body' => [
                                'Against a soprano, look at the top. Many sopranos sing around F5 to A5 with ease; a mezzo can often reach those notes but tires if a song stays there, and often feels the voice change gear a note or two lower. Against an alto, look at the bottom: an alto\'s notes around F3 to A3 come out full, where a mezzo\'s are lighter.',
                                'Colour is the other clue: a mezzo sounds warmer and darker than a soprano, but lighter than an alto. Many voices sit right on these lines, and that\'s fine. The vocal range test gives a first guess, and singing both choir parts will tell you more.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing mezzo-soprano in a choir',
                            'body' => [
                                'Choirs have no mezzo part, so a mezzo sings Soprano 2 or Alto 1: the lower soprano line or the higher alto line when those parts split. In plain four-part music, a mezzo joins whichever section needs voices, often alto for a lower mezzo and soprano for a higher one.',
                                'Soprano 2 sings a harmony just under the tune, and Alto 1 sits above the low alto notes, so a mezzo often sings the notes in the middle of the chord. Much of the middle of the chord sits near the change of gear a few notes above middle C, so keep it light there and let the sound mix, rather than pushing your chest voice up.',
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['What is the mezzo-soprano range?', 'The usual mezzo-soprano range is A3 to F5, from the A below middle C to the F an octave and a fourth above it. Trained mezzos often go higher, and every voice is a little different.'],
                        ['Should a mezzo sing soprano or alto in a choir?', 'It depends on your colour as much as your range: a mezzo usually sings Soprano 2 or Alto 1. A brighter, lighter mezzo often blends best with the sopranos, a warmer, darker one with the altos. Try both, and let the director hear you.'],
                        ['Is a mezzo-soprano higher than an alto?', 'Yes, a little: the mezzo\'s usual range, A3 to F5, sits a few notes above the alto\'s F3 to D5. Most of the notes are shared, so comfort and colour tell them apart more than range does.'],
                        ['Can a mezzo sing soprano songs?', 'Many fit as written, because soprano songs often sit in the middle and only touch the top. When the high notes linger, sing it a step or two lower: the tune stays the same. Keep the top light rather than pushing for it.'],
                    ],
                ],
                'soprano' => [
                    'name' => 'Soprano',
                    'summary' => 'The highest voice, bright and clear, usually singing the tune.',
                    'range' => 'C4 to A5',
                    'eyebrow' => 'Usual range C4 to A5 · sings Soprano',
                    'title' => 'Soprano Vocal Range: Notes, Part and Tips',
                    'description' => 'The soprano\'s usual range is C4 to A5. Hear its notes, see how to tell a soprano from a mezzo-soprano, and learn what the soprano part does in a choir.',
                    'headline' => 'The soprano voice',
                    'lead' => 'The soprano is the highest of the usual voices, with a usual range from C4 to A5. It sounds bright and clear at the top, and in a choir it usually sings the tune.',
                    'guide' => [
                        [
                            'heading' => 'The soprano range',
                            'body' => [
                                'A soprano\'s usual range runs from C4, middle C, to A5, the A an octave and a sixth above it: 880 Hz, an octave above the note an orchestra tunes to. Many sopranos are most at home in the upper part of it, from about F4 to G5, where lower voices start to tire.',
                                'Trained sopranos often reach C6, the note singers call a soprano\'s high C, and some light voices go higher still. Below middle C most sopranos have a few notes, but they get quiet.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Am I a soprano or a mezzo-soprano?',
                            'body' => [
                                'The ranges share everything from C4 to F5, so look at where singing stays easy. A soprano can sing a song that sits around F5 and G5 without tiring; a mezzo usually can\'t for long. Many sopranos feel the voice change gear twice: once from chest to head voice a few notes above middle C, and again near the top of the staff, around F5, where a mezzo\'s second change often comes a note or two lower.',
                                'Colour helps too: a soprano sounds brighter and lighter, a mezzo warmer and darker in the middle. Your top note alone doesn\'t settle it, because many mezzos can reach A5. If the soprano part is easy all evening, try soprano; if it tires you, try Soprano 2 or alto.',
                            ],
                        ],
                        [
                            'heading' => 'Singing soprano in a choir',
                            'body' => [
                                'In most choir music the sopranos sing the tune, so listeners hear their part first and the other parts fit around it. When the part divides, Soprano 1 takes the higher line and Soprano 2 a harmony just below.',
                                'Choir soprano parts mostly sit from about D4 to G5 and seldom go above A5. Sing the high notes in a light, open head voice rather than pushing chest voice up: they carry better, tune better and blend with the choir. Stop if it strains; a teacher can help.',
                            ],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'faq' => [
                        ['What is the highest note a soprano can sing?', 'The usual soprano range tops out at A5, the A an octave and a sixth above middle C. Trained sopranos often reach C6, called a soprano\'s high C, and some light voices go higher. Choir soprano parts seldom go above A5.'],
                        ['Am I a soprano or an alto?', 'Check your top, and how long you can stay there. A soprano sits comfortably between about F4 and G5 through a whole song; if notes that high tire you quickly and F3 to D5 feels easy, alto is likely the better fit. A mezzo-soprano sits between the two.'],
                        ['Can a man sing soprano?', 'Yes, though it is rare: a few countertenors sing in the soprano range, in head voice or falsetto. Boys sing soprano before their voices change, which is why many church choirs have boy trebles on the top line.'],
                        ['Do sopranos always sing the tune?', 'Usually, but not always. In most hymns and choir songs the soprano part is the tune, but some arrangements give it to the altos, tenors or basses for a verse, and the sopranos then sing a harmony above or around it.'],
                    ],
                ],
            ],
        ],
