                    'Answer a call with a line of your own: its rhythm kept, its notes your choice.',
                    'Sing the blue note and the blues scale, and scat a chorus of your own.',
                ],
                'units_say' => [
                    'Long, short: the swing feel, phrases that start on the "and", and your first trades with the band.',
                    'The twelve bars, the chord\'s 1, 3 and 5 to land on, and the third, the colour note.',
                    'Keep the rhythm and change the end, then whole answers, a riff, and trading twos.',
                    'The blue note, the blues scale, four bars of your own, then a chorus.',
                ],
                'who' => [
                    'Singers who\'ve sung a harmony part and want to make up a line of their own.',
                    'Anyone who loves a jazz singer trading with the band and wants to try it.',
                    'Choir and a cappella singers who have never improvised and want a safe place to start.',
                ],
                'need' => [
                    'Headphones, so the mic hears you and not the band.',
                    'A quiet spot and a few minutes at a time.',
                    'No reading music and no jazz theory: the notes that fit are lit.',
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['Do I need to know jazz?', 'No. Lesson 1 starts with the band playing a short call and you echoing it on syllables. The notes that fit are lit, so there are no wrong ones to hunt for.'],
                    ['What are the syllables for?', 'They make your voice speak like a horn: a soft "doo" on the long note, a leaning "bah" on the short one, a clean "dat" to end. The app hears your notes and your timing, not which syllables you choose.'],
                    ['Is scat the same as vocalese?', 'No. Scat is made up on the spot, on syllables. Vocalese sets words to a band\'s recorded solos and horn parts, as on Sing a Song of Basie.'],
                    ['Is it safe for my voice?', 'Yes, sung the course\'s way: light, at a medium volume, in the middle of your voice, with accents from the breath and never the throat. Stop if anything tightens or hurts.'],
                    ['What does it cost?', 'Nothing: every course is free. Try the first lesson without an account; a free account opens the rest.'],
                ],
                'related' => ['improvising-harmony', 'ear-training', 'pop-and-country'],
                'kicks' => ['story' => 'The story', 'aims' => 'Long, short', 'units' => 'The lessons', 'science' => 'The science', 'margin' => 'Liner notes', 'begin' => 'Before you begin', 'questions' => 'Questions', 'close' => 'Your first trade'],
                'close' => 'Lesson 1 is a trade: the band plays two bars, you sing them back on syllables, and end on any lit note.',
                'story_lead' => 'Five records, from the blues\' twelve bars to three singers stacking their own voices into a band.',
                'love' => [
                    'eyebrow' => 'A free course · :lessons lessons',
                    'chips' => [':units units, :lessons lessons', 'Every note that fits is lit', 'Ends with a chorus of your own'],
                    // The hero's chart: the twelve-bar blues in C, a chord a bar, coloured by its job.
                    'chart' => [
                        'title' => 'The twelve bars',
                        'bars' => ['C7', 'C7', 'C7', 'C7', 'F7', 'F7', 'C7', 'C7', 'G7', 'F7', 'C7', 'G7'],
                        'bar_n' => 'Bar :n',
                        'numerals' => ['home' => 'I', 'away' => 'IV', 'turn' => 'V'],
                        'legend' => ['home' => 'I, home (C7)', 'away' => 'IV, away (F7)', 'turn' => 'V, the turn back (G7)'],
                        'note' => 'W. C. Handy opened "St. Louis Blues", in 1914, with this standard twelve-bar plan.',
                        'alt' => 'The twelve-bar blues in C, four bars to a row: C7 four times; F7 twice and C7 twice; then G7, F7, C7 and G7.',
                    ],
                    // Straight or swung? (js/public/scat.js): the same eight notes in three feels.
                    'demo' => [
                        'live' => 'Live demo',
                        'lesson' => 'Lesson 2 · Long, short',
                        'title' => 'Straight or swung?',
                        'lead' => 'One scat line, eight notes up a C7 chord and back, at 120 beats a minute. Straight gives every note the same length. Swung makes each pair long, short, 2 to 1. Soloist is lighter, under 2 to 1, with each long note a touch late, the way soloists sit against the drummer. Same notes every time: only the timing changes.',
                        'play' => 'Play the line',
                        'stop' => 'Stop',
                        'feel' => 'Feel',
                        'feels' => ['straight' => 'Straight', 'swung' => 'Swung', 'soloist' => 'Soloist'],
                        'readout' => ['straight' => 'Straight · 250 + 250 ms', 'swung' => 'Swung · 333 + 167 ms, 2 to 1', 'soloist' => 'Soloist · 300 + 200 ms, the long note 33 ms late'],
                        'playing' => ['straight' => 'Straight: every note the same length.', 'swung' => 'Swung: long, short, doo-bah.', 'soloist' => 'Soloist: lighter, leaning back on the beat, together on the "and".'],
                        'ready' => 'Pick a feel and play the line. Watch the notes move when the feel changes.',
                        'after' => 'Same notes, same speed. Try another feel.',
                        'syllables' => ['doo', 'bah', 'doo', 'bah', 'doo', 'bah', 'doo', 'dat'],
                        'notes' => ['C', 'E', 'G', 'A', 'B♭', 'A', 'G', 'E'],
                        'beats' => ['1', '2', '3', '4'],
                        'and' => '&',
                        'lane' => 'The line\'s eight notes, placed by when each starts and how long it lasts; higher notes sit higher.',
                        'note_n' => 'Note :n, :syllable on :note',
                        'note' => 'The syllables are the kind a scat singer uses; the synth voice sings every note on "ah". The line plays twice over a soft C7 chord. The soloist\'s 33 ms is our own choice: the study measured the pattern, not this number. No mic here; in the course, the app listens as you sing.',
                    ],
                    // The story, from the research pack (docs/courses/scat-love-letter.md): [when, title, text, notes].
                    'story' => [
                        ['1914', 'Twelve bars on Beale Street', 'W. C. Handy, the "Father of the Blues", was already 40 when he wrote "St. Louis Blues" in Memphis, "pulling an all-nighter in a rented room". It "opened with the standard 12-bar blues format", then turned to a tango rhythm in the bridge.', ['handy']],
                        ['1927', 'A conversation with a guitar', 'In Chicago, Louis Armstrong\'s Hot Five recorded "Hotter Than That", a tune by their pianist, Lil Hardin Armstrong, with Lonnie Johnson sitting in on guitar. Armstrong sings a scat solo, then he and Johnson trade phrases, a "musical conversation between voice and guitar".', ['okeh', 'asu', 'hotfive']],
                        ['1931', 'Hi-de-ho', 'Cab Calloway\'s orchestra recorded "Minnie the Moocher", "The ho de ho song" on the label. Calloway sang his scat "in a rapid call-and-response style" and had the audience answer him, a game that got harder to follow once his nonsense syllables came in.', ['brunswick', 'minnie']],
                        ['1945', 'A voice among the horns', 'Ella Fitzgerald recorded "Flying Home" with Vic Schoen\'s orchestra of trumpets, trombone, saxophones and rhythm. The National Endowment for the Arts calls her scat, "using the technique of a master instrumental improviser", her hallmark.', ['decca', 'ella']],
                        ['1957', 'Three voices, a whole band', 'Dave Lambert and Jon Hendricks hired singers for Count Basie\'s band arrangements, with Annie Ross to coach them. "They hit every note perfectly, but it didn\'t swing," she remembered. So the three sang the many parts themselves, "one on top of the other", a technique "rarely used back then". Ross: "It was MAGIC." Sing a Song of Basie, a hit in 1958, is vocalese, not scat: words set to the band\'s solos. Its idea is this app\'s too: one voice at a time, stacked into a band.', ['moment', 'ross', 'hendricks', 'ecu']],
                    ],
                    // The science, in plain words: [title, text, notes].
                    'science' => [
                        ['Swing, measured', 'Researchers measured classic jazz group recordings. At medium tempos the drummer\'s long-short pairs were always longer than 2 to 1, and the soloist\'s always shorter. The soloist also sat a little behind the drummer on the beat, and the two landed together on the "and". That\'s the demo\'s Soloist.', ['kth']],
                        ['Swing isn\'t sloppiness', 'A team recorded a professional pianist playing twelve jazz standards, then took the tiny timing wobbles out of his notes, doubled them, or flipped them, and 160 listeners rated the swing. The cleaned-up versions rated slightly higher than the originals, and the doubled ones lowest. The long-short pattern carries the swing, not the wobble.', ['datseris']],
                        ['Trading is a conversation', 'Trading fours means taking turns, four bars each, each player answering the last. A 2014 study scanned eleven jazz pianists trading with a partner. Compared with swapping memorised phrases, trading raised activity in language areas tied to grammar and lowered it in areas tied to word meaning: a musical conversation uses the brain\'s grammar, not its dictionary.', ['donnay']],
                        ['A note between the keys', 'A piano offers a minor third or a major third, and nothing between. A 2019 study measured the sung pitches in fifteen classic blues recordings and found a "neutral" third among them, between the two keys, and a flat third sung a little higher than the piano\'s. A voice can sing where a keyboard can\'t: that\'s the blue note.', ['cutting']],
                    ],
                    // Liner notes (the pack's surprising facts, none told in the app): [text, notes].
                    'margin' => [
                        ['Louis Armstrong\'s Hot Five was "united only for recording purposes": in its whole life it played live only twice.', ['hotfive']],
                        ['When W. C. Handy first recorded, in 1917, the clarinetist played from a six-foot stool into a megaphone near the ceiling, and "the drums and basses could not be recorded in those days".', ['handy']],
                        ['"Minnie the Moocher", hi-de-ho and all, sold more than a million copies.', ['minnie']],
                        ['Ella Fitzgerald was the first singer to receive the NEA Jazz Masters Fellowship, in 1985. She started out in the amateur contest at the Apollo Theater, at 17.', ['ella']],
                        ['For their scanner study of trading fours, the scientists wrote their own tune for the pianists to learn, "Tradewinds", a twelve-bar blues, over a backing track at 144 beats a minute.', ['donnay']],
                        ['Offered chord progressions to rate, jazz musicians liked the ones that went somewhere unexpected, and their brainwaves reacted more strongly to the surprises.', ['przysinda']],
                    ],
                    // The sources (docs/courses/scat-love-letter.md), numbered in this order. The Library of
                    // Congress essays are linked as read, through their Internet Archive copies.
                    'sources' => [
                        'handy' => ['Library of Congress, National Recording Registry, "St. Louis Blues" (Internet Archive copy)', 'https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.loc.gov/static/programs/national-recording-preservation-board/documents/St-Louis-Blues_Freeland.pdf'],
                        'okeh' => ['Discography of American Historical Recordings, OKeh matrix W82055, "Hotter Than That"', 'https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/index.php/matrix/detail/2000208714'],
                        'asu' => ['Arizona State University, MUS 354, Armstrong listening guides (pages 29 to 30)', 'https://provost.asu.edu/sites/g/files/litvpz671/files/gsc/2626/mus_354_mandatory_review_hu.pdf'],
                        'hotfive' => ['Library of Congress, National Recording Registry, Hot Five and Hot Seven Recordings (Internet Archive copy)', 'https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.loc.gov/static/programs/national-recording-preservation-board/documents/HotFiveHotSevenRecordings.pdf'],
                        'brunswick' => ['Discography of American Historical Recordings, Brunswick matrix E36212, "Minnie the Moocher"', 'https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/index.php/matrix/detail/2000358948'],
                        'minnie' => ['Library of Congress, National Recording Registry, "Minnie the Moocher" (Internet Archive copy)', 'https://web.archive.org/web/2025/https://www.loc.gov/static/programs/national-recording-preservation-board/documents/MinnieTheMoocher.pdf'],
                        'decca' => ['Discography of American Historical Recordings, Decca matrix 73066, "Flying Home"', 'https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/index.php/matrix/detail/2000301283'],
                        'ella' => ['National Endowment for the Arts, Ella Fitzgerald, 1985 NEA Jazz Master', 'https://www.arts.gov/honors/jazz/ella-fitzgerald'],
                        'moment' => ['National Endowment for the Arts, Jazz Moments, recording "Sing a Song of Basie" (2013)', 'https://www.arts.gov/stories/jazz-moments/annie-ross-recording-sing-song-basie'],
