                    'note' => 'In lesson 1 you sing the line yourself, plain and then with a scoop, and hear your two takes back.',
                    'hear' => 'Hear it',
                ],
            ],
            'a-cappella-bass' => [
                'group' => 'harmony',
                'name' => 'A cappella bass',
                'summary' => 'Low lines and holding a groove.',
                'title' => 'A cappella bass course',
                'description' => 'Low lines and holding a groove. A free harmony course on Stackapella, in short lessons.',
                'headline' => 'A cappella bass',
                'lead' => 'Low lines that hold the whole group up, with "dm dm" syllables and a steady groove.',
                'learn' => ['Low lines, in tune', 'Bass syllables', 'Holding the groove'],
                'measures' => 'The app hears each note\'s pitch and when it starts, whether a long note stops on its beat, whether you come back in on time after a rest, and whether a held root stays put. It hears down to 60 Hz, well under the lowest note here. It can\'t hear loudness honestly, so balance is a check you make yourself.',
                'safety' => ['Low and easy: never press, growl or tuck your chin to reach a note.', 'Keep your throat where it is when you speak; don\'t push the voice darker or louder to sound like a bass.', 'If it rumbles or tickles, sing it higher: the app follows. Low notes use more air, so take a breath every two bars.'],
                'songs' => [['Swing Low, Sweet Chariot', 'Spiritual, public domain'], ['The four-chord lines and grooves', 'Written for the course']],
                // The designed page (site/courses/a-cappella-bass): its own sections and words, and in `love`
                // its demos, its counts from the course file (:lessons, :units) and its cited facts.
                'app' => 'a-cappella-bass',
                'eyebrow' => 'A free course',
                'hero_title' => 'Be the floor.',
                'hero_lead' => 'In a cappella there\'s no bass guitar and no piano\'s left hand: the bass is a voice. You sing the chord\'s root, the note the whole group stands on, in "doo", "doom" and "dm", with a groove that starts and stops on the beat.',
                'see_lessons' => 'See the lessons',
                'titles' => [
                    'story' => 'A short history of the bottom line',
                    'outcomes' => 'By the end, you can',
                    'units' => 'From one root to a whole song\'s bass',
                    'science' => 'Why the bass matters, in plain words',
                    'margin' => 'Notes in the margin',
                    'inside' => 'What the app listens for',
                    'who' => 'Who it\'s for',
                    'need' => 'What you need',
                    'safety' => 'Singing it safely',
                    'faq' => 'Questions',
                    'join' => 'Hold the group up.',
                    'related' => 'Good with this course',
                ],
                'outcomes' => [
                    'Sing low notes easily, without pressing, down to the floor of a line.',
                    'Find each chord\'s root by ear and sing it under the group.',
                    'Sing "doom", "dm" and "buh" so each note starts and stops on the beat.',
                    'Hold a root-and-fifth groove, walk between roots, and sing Swing Low\'s bass under three voices.',
                ],
                'units_say' => [
                    'The root under a chord, low and easy notes, bright or dark by ear, and the four chords\' roots.',
                    'Doom, dm and buh: notes that stop on time, call and response, and talking like a bass.',
                    'Root and fifth, keeping time for the group, hearing a bass that rushes, and a groove with rests.',
                    'Walking between roots, arriving home on the beat, and Swing Low\'s bass with the group.',
                ],
                'who' => [
                    'Anyone who sings in a group and wants to hold it up from underneath.',
                    'A cappella and barbershop singers moving to bass, or trying it for the first time.',
                    'Higher voices too: every line sits an octave up for you, as in women\'s barbershop.',
                ],
                'need' => [
                    'Headphones, wired if you have them, so you hear the group over you.',
                    'A few minutes at a time: each lesson takes 5 minutes or less.',
                    'No reading music and no low voice: every line is laid from your own comfortable note.',
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['Do I need a low voice?', 'No. Every line is laid from your own comfortable note, so the bass sits in the low middle of the voice that sings it: a bass sings it where a bass does, a higher voice an octave up, as a women\'s barbershop chorus does. Nothing goes more than a fifth below your comfortable note.'],
                    ['Is it safe for my voice?', 'Yes. Nothing is held at the bottom of your range: the lowest notes come only as short notes inside a line. Never press, growl or tuck your chin; if a note rumbles, sing it higher and the app follows. No vocal fry is taught.'],
                    ['Will the app hear my low notes?', 'Yes. It hears down to 60 Hz, well under the lowest note in the course. Low notes are quieter, so don\'t push: let the mic do the work.'],
                    ['Do I need to read music?', 'No. You learn each line by ear from a guide bass, then sing it on your own under three guide voices.'],
                    ['What does it cost?', 'Nothing: every course is free. Try the first lesson without an account; a free account opens the rest.'],
                ],
                'related' => ['barbershop', 'ear-training', 'counterpoint'],
                'kicks' => ['story' => 'The story', 'aims' => 'The floor', 'units' => 'The lessons', 'science' => 'The science', 'margin' => 'Margin notes', 'begin' => 'Before you begin', 'questions' => 'Questions', 'close' => 'Your first root'],
                'close' => 'Lesson 1 takes a few minutes: hear a chord with no floor, then with one, then sing the root under three voices yourself.',
                'love' => [
                    'eyebrow' => 'A free course · :lessons lessons',
                    'chips' => [':units units, :lessons lessons', 'For any voice, from your own comfortable note', 'Ends with Swing Low\'s bass under three voices'],
                    // The hero's card: a bass C's first six harmonics, spaced as the ear hears them.
                    'overtones' => [
                        'title' => 'One bass note, six harmonics',
                        'alt' => 'A bass C and its first six harmonics as rungs, the lowest widest: C, C an octave up, G, C, E and G. Harmonics 3 and 5, a G and an E, are the rest of a C major chord.',
                        'names' => ['C', 'C', 'G', 'C', 'E', 'G'],
                        'floor' => 'The bass note, C',
                        'octave' => 'C again, octaves up',
                        'chord' => 'G and E: the rest of C major',
                        'note' => 'For a bass C, harmonics 3 and 5 are a G and an E: the rest of a C major chord. When the bass sings the root, the notes above it line up with its own overtones.',
                    ],
                    // Same notes, new root (js/public/a-cappella-bass.js), from the pack's first demo.
                    'demo' => [
                        'live' => 'Live demo',
                        'lesson' => 'From lesson 3 · Bright or dark?',
                        'title' => 'Same notes, new root',
                        'lead' => 'Two notes hold still at the top, C and the E above it. Under them the bass sings C, then A. The top never moves, but the chord turns from bright C major to darker A minor. The bass decides what the chord is.',
                        'play' => 'Hear the bass change',
                        'stop' => 'Stop',
                        'stage' => 'The three chords, each one a button to hear it.',
                        // [name, mood] for each chord: the top notes alone, over C, over A.
                        'chords' => [['No bass', 'Just the top'], ['C major', 'Bright'], ['A minor', 'Darker']],
                        'top' => ['C4', 'E4'],
                        'bass' => ['', 'C3', 'A2'],
                        'empty' => 'No floor',
                        'says' => [
                            'Just the top two notes, C and E: no floor yet.',
                            'The bass sings C under them: C major, bright.',
                            'Now the bass sings A. The same two notes on top: A minor, darker.',
                        ],
                        'ready' => 'Press play: the top two notes alone, then the bass on C, then on A.',
                        'after' => 'Tap a chord to hear it again, and flip between the two basses.',
                        'hz' => 'On top, C4 (262 Hz) and E4 (330 Hz); the bass, C3 (131 Hz) then A2 (110 Hz).',
                        'note' => 'No mic here: the demo only plays. Each bass note is built from its harmonics, so even a phone speaker can sound it.',
                    ],
                    // The note that isn't there: G2 from harmonics 1 to 6, then without the first.
                    'missing' => [
                        'title' => 'The note that isn\'t there',
                        'lead' => 'The ear hears a pitch from the pattern of the harmonics, not only from the lowest one. In one lab\'s words, "the pitch of a harmonic complex is the same whether or not the fundamental frequency is present". This is the missing fundamental. It is why a bass line still comes through small speakers that "cannot reproduce the full range of frequencies".',
                        'try' => 'An organ tone on G2 built from six harmonics: 98, 196, 294, 392, 490 and 588 Hz. Then the 98 Hz goes, and comes back. Most people still hear G2. On a phone speaker the 98 Hz may never have been there at all, which is the point.',
                        'play' => 'Hear it with and without',
                        'stop' => 'Stop',
                        'with' => 'With 98 Hz',
                        'without' => '98 Hz gone',
                        'after' => 'Most people still hear G2 with the 98 Hz gone.',
                    ],
                    // The story, from the research pack (docs/courses/a-cappella-bass-love-letter.md): [when, title, text, notes].
                    'story' => [
                        ['1722', 'The bass that rules the chord', 'Jean-Philippe Rameau\'s Treatise on Harmony came out in Paris and built harmony on what he called the fundamental bass. A London translation of its third part (1779) put it plainly: the "grand Art or Mystery in Composition" lies "in the Bass, which we call Fundamental".', ['rameau', 'rameau1779']],
                        ['1871', 'Two basses on the road', 'The first Fisk Jubilee Singers left Nashville on 6 October 1871: students singing to help their university "in the time of its necessity". Their 1872 songbook lists them by part: three sopranos, two contraltos, two tenors, and two basses, Isaac P. Dickerson and Greene Evans. Seven of the nine had been enslaved. The songs were written out for the first time by Theodore F. Seward and "sold by hundreds at their concerts".', ['fisk', 'marsh']],
                        ['1909', 'Half the quartet sings bass', 'In December 1909, in Camden, New Jersey, four Fisk students recorded spirituals for Victor with no accompaniment. Two of the four were basses, Alfred Garfield King and Noah Ryder, under two tenors. Their "Swing Low, Sweet Chariot" is the first known recording of the song.', ['dahr1909', 'pdr']],
                        ['1937', 'The jubilee quartet', 'On 4 August 1937, in Charlotte, North Carolina, the Golden Gate Jubilee Quartet recorded for Bluebird as a "male vocal quartet, unaccompanied", the traditional "Go Where I Send Thee" among the songs. The discography lists two tenors, a baritone and a bass. The baritone, Orlandus Wilson, became the group\'s bass and stayed until 1998.', ['dahrgg', 'dahrgo', 'dahrwilson']],
                        ['1946', 'The bass takes the lead', 'The Ravens, started by two Harlem waiters, one of them the bass Jimmy Ricks, were the first group to make "continuous use of a bass vocalist" on lead. At the Apollo, when Ricks took over the lead, "his booming bass brought the house down". A bass singing lead became their trademark, and one of their "most often emulated attributes over the next decade".', ['ravens']],
                    ],
                    // The science, in plain words: [title, text, notes]; the missing fundamental, with its demo, comes after the second.
                    'science' => [
                        ['Why the bass sounds like the root', 'A sung note is not one frequency. It is a stack of harmonics at very nearly 2, 3, 4 and 5 times the lowest one. For a bass C, harmonics 3 and 5 are a G and an E: the rest of a C major chord. So when the bass sings the root, the notes above it line up with the bass\'s own overtones. That is why the Barbershop Harmony Society tells arrangers: "Give the bass the root or fifth".', ['unsw', 'bhs8']],
                        ['Low notes are hard to hear, and hard to push', 'Our hearing is most sensitive around 1 to 4 kHz, so a bass note at 98 Hz (G2) needs more sound to seem as loud. The voice doesn\'t help: its range of loudness shrinks at the bottom of its range, and pushing more air into a low note tends to make the pitch creep up. At low pitches, a brighter sound, with more energy in the upper harmonics, makes a note louder.', ['unswhear', 'titze']],
                        ['The bass keeps time', 'In a 2014 study, listeners heard a high and a low stream of tones together. Now and then one tone came 50 milliseconds early. The brain\'s response to the slip was larger when the low tone slipped, and people\'s tapping followed the low stream more. A model traced the effect to the inner ear. The authors say this explains why bass instruments usually carry the rhythm, while melody sits on top.', ['pnas']],
                        ['Tuning from the bottom', 'Pure intervals are simple ratios: a fifth is 3:2 and a major third is 5:4. When two upper notes form a pure major third, the ear can hear a faint low "Tartini tone" exactly two octaves below the lower note. Players tune so that this phantom note agrees with a lower instrument. In an a cappella group, the lower instrument is the bass.', ['tartini']],
                    ],
                    // Notes in the margin (the pack's surprising facts): [text, notes].
                    'margin' => [
                        ['Writing down the Fisk songs in 1872, Theodore Seward found none in triple time. He put it down to "the beating of the foot and the swaying of the body", which keep an even beat.', ['fisk']],
                        ['A barbershop chorus aims for about 40 percent of its singers on bass, 30 on lead, 20 on baritone and 10 on tenor; many go as high as half on bass.', ['bhsabout']],
                        ['Women\'s barbershop has basses too. In SSAA arrangements the bass part is written in an "octave bass clef" and sung an octave higher than printed.', ['bhsparts']],
                        ['Babies start hearing the missing fundamental between 3 and 4 months of age: four-month-olds and adults showed a brain response to the pitch changes, and 3-month-olds did not.', ['jneuro']],
                        ['Chinchillas hear the missing fundamental too: trained animals treated tones with and without their lowest harmonic as the same.', ['jaro']],
                    ],
                    // The sources (docs/courses/a-cappella-bass-love-letter.md), numbered in this order.
                    'sources' => [
                        'unsw' => ['UNSW, "How harmonic are harmonics?"', 'https://newt.phys.unsw.edu.au/jw/harmonics.html'],
                        'bhs8' => ['Barbershop Harmony Society, "8 ways to start arranging barbershop"', 'https://www.barbershop.org/8-ways-to-start-arranging-barbershop'],
                        'rameau' => ['archive.org, Traité de l\'harmonie (Paris, Ballard, 1722)', 'https://archive.org/details/traitdelharmon00rame'],
                        'rameau1779' => ['archive.org, A Treatise of Music (London, 1779), "Of the Fundamental Bass"', 'https://archive.org/details/treatiseofmusicc00rame'],
                        'fisk' => ['archive.org, Jubilee Songs as sung by the Jubilee Singers of Fisk University (New York, 1872)', 'https://archive.org/details/jubileesongsassu00jubi'],
                        'marsh' => ['archive.org, J. B. T. Marsh, The Story of the Jubilee Singers (1876)', 'https://archive.org/details/storyofjubileesi00mars_3'],
                        'dahr1909' => ['Discography of American Historical Recordings, Victor B-8450 (1909)', 'https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/index.php/matrix/detail/200008526/B-8450-Little_David_play_on_yo_harp'],
                        'pdr' => ['The Public Domain Review, "First recording of Swing Low, Sweet Chariot (1909)"', 'https://publicdomainreview.org/collections/first-recording-of-swing-low-sweet-chariot-1909/'],
                        'dahrgg' => ['Discography of American Historical Recordings, Victor BS-011931 (1937)', 'https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/index.php/matrix/detail/200028565/BS-011931-Golden_Gate_Gospel_Train'],
                        'dahrgo' => ['Discography of American Historical Recordings, Victor BS-011938 (1937)', 'https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/index.php/matrix/detail/200028572/BS-011938-Go_where_I_send_thee'],
                        'dahrwilson' => ['Discography of American Historical Recordings, Orlandus "Dad" Wilson', 'https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/index.php/mastertalent/detail/351208/Wilson_Orlandus_Dad'],
                        'ravens' => ['Vocal Group Hall of Fame, "The Ravens"', 'https://vocalgroup.org/inductees/the-ravens/'],
                        'unswhear' => ['UNSW, "Hearing curves and equal loudness"', 'https://newt.phys.unsw.edu.au/jw/hearing.html'],
                        'titze' => ['Journal of Speech and Hearing Research (1992), the voice range profile', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1735970/'],
                        'jasa' => ['Journal of the Acoustical Society of America (2012), the missing fundamental', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3528754/'],
                        'libre' => ['LibreTexts, Sound: An Interactive eBook, "Missing Fundamental"', 'https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Acoustics/Book%3A_Sound_-_An_Interactive_eBook_(Forinash_and_Christian)/09%3A_The_Ear_and_Perception/9.01%3A_The_Ear_and_Perception/9.1.06%3A_Missing_Fundamental_Simulation'],
                        'pnas' => ['PNAS (2014), the low voice and the beat', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4104866/'],
                        'tartini' => ['UNSW, "Tartini tones, consonance, temperament"', 'https://animations.physics.unsw.edu.au/jw/Tartini-tones-temperament.html'],
                        'bhsabout' => ['Barbershop Harmony Society, "What is barbershop music?"', 'https://www.barbershop.org/music/about-our-music'],
                        'bhsparts' => ['Barbershop Harmony Society, "Barbershop Voice Parts and Voicings" (PDF)', 'https://files.barbershop.org/PDFs/Education/Barbershop-Voice-Parts-Voicing.pdf'],
                        'jneuro' => ['Journal of Neuroscience (2009), infants and the missing fundamental', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6665621/'],
                        'jaro' => ['JARO (2011), chinchillas and the missing fundamental', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3015027/'],
                    ],
                ],
            ],
            'other-languages' => [
                'group' => 'skills',
                'name' => 'Singing in another language',
                'summary' => 'Four songs in their own languages, sounds first.',
                'title' => 'Singing in another language course',
                'description' => 'Four songs in Japanese, Spanish, church Latin and Ukrainian, sounds first. A free singing skills course coming to Stackapella, in short lessons.',
                'headline' => 'Sing in another language',
                'lead' => 'A song\'s sounds and rhythm first, by ear, then the words, in Japanese, Spanish, church Latin and Ukrainian.',
                'learn' => ['Every vowel kept whole', 'Every small sound on its own note', 'A song in each language'],
                // The designed page (site/courses/other-languages): its own sections and words. The app hears pitch and
