                'app' => 'other-languages',
                'hero_title' => 'Sounds first, then words.',
                'hero_lead' => 'Sing four songs from the library in their own languages: Sakura Sakura in Japanese, La Paloma in Spanish, Mozart\'s Ave verum corpus in church Latin and Shchedryk in Ukrainian. You learn them the way choirs do: the tune, then its vowels, then the words, a line at a time, by ear, knowing what they mean.',
                'hero_facts' => ['Five minutes or less a lesson', 'Four public-domain songs', 'No reading music'],
                'see_lessons' => 'See the lessons',
                'see_aims' => 'What you\'ll learn',
                'titles' => [
                    'outcomes' => 'By the end, you can',
                    'units' => 'Four songs, four languages, harder each time',
                    'try' => 'Sakura, in three minutes',
                    'inside' => 'What the app listens for',
                    'who' => 'Who it\'s for',
                    'need' => 'What you need',
                    'safety' => 'Singing it safely',
                    'faq' => 'Questions',
                    'join' => 'Sa-ku-ra, not sa-ku-ruh.',
                    'related' => 'Good with this course',
                ],
                'outcomes' => [
                    'Keep every vowel whole, with no "uh" on the quiet syllables.',
                    'Give every small sound its own note, or join two words\' vowels on one, as the language asks.',
                    'Sing a long Latin line on one breath, breathing only between words.',
                    'Hold the key through Shchedryk\'s figure as it gets faster, then sing its ending from the Cyrillic with the choir.',
                ],
                'units_say' => [
                    'Sakura Sakura in Japanese: five whole vowels, every small sound on its own note and a hummed n, then the song with the library\'s other parts.',
                    'La Paloma in Spanish: the habanera\'s rhythm, two words\' vowels on one note, a tapped r and a soft b, then its refrain.',
                    'Ave verum corpus, "Hail, true body", in church Latin: an open e, a soft c and g, a silent h and a breath only between words, then its opening with the other three parts.',
                    'Shchedryk in Ukrainian: its letters in Cyrillic, the figure faster with the key held, the swallow\'s line, then its ending in Cyrillic with the choir.',
                ],
                'try' => [
                    'lead' => 'The first lesson puts you in a choir singing Japanese.',
                    'steps' => [
                        'One card: さくら さくら, "sa-ku-ra, sa-ku-ra", cherry blossoms.',
                        'Sing the first line on its vowels only, "a u a, a u a", then with its words.',
                        'Sing lines 1 and 2 with the library\'s other parts, in a key chosen for your voice.',
                    ],
                    'after' => 'The app scores the pitch and the beat. Whether the last a of "sakura" stayed "ah" is for your own ear.',
                ],
                'measures' => 'The app hears pitch and timing, and nothing else. It scores a steady held note, every syllable sung on its own note and on the beat (a swallowed or whispered one leaves its note silent), a phrase on one breath, and the key held through Shchedryk\'s figure. It can\'t tell which vowel or consonant you made. Vowel colour, the tapped r, two vowels joined on one note and the silent h are yours to check: after a good take you play it back and answer a plain question, with a written sound guide beside it.',
                'scoring' => ['game' => 'Up to three stars and XP a lesson, for notes on pitch and on the beat.', 'serious' => 'The same lesson shows the plain measure, with no stars or rewards.'],
                'who' => [
                    'Choir singers with a song in another language coming up.',
                    'Anyone who has sung a foreign word and wondered how it should sound.',
                ],
                'need' => [
                    'A quiet spot where you can sing at a medium volume.',
                    'No reading music, and none of the four languages: every line comes with its meaning.',
                ],
                'safety' => [
                    'An easy jaw and a soft tongue: new sounds come from the tongue and lips, never from pushing.',
                    'A medium volume, in the middle of your voice. Start vowel words gently, with no catch in the throat, and stop if anything tightens.',
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['Does the app hear my words?', 'No. It hears pitch and timing: steady notes, every syllable on its own note and on the beat, a phrase on one breath, and the key held through Shchedryk\'s figure. Which vowel or consonant you made is for your own ear: you play your take back and answer a plain question, with a written sound guide beside it.'],
                    ['Which songs does it use?', 'Four from the library, all public domain: Sakura Sakura (Japanese), La Paloma by Sebastián Iradier, about 1863 (Spanish), Mozart\'s Ave verum corpus (church Latin) and Shchedryk, a traditional Ukrainian shchedrivka, the New Year song Mykola Leontovych made famous in his 1916 choral setting.'],
                    ['Is Shchedryk the tune I know as "Carol of the Bells"?', 'Yes, it\'s the same tune. Here you sing it in Ukrainian, in our own arrangement of the folk tune.'],
                    ['Do I need to speak the languages?', 'No. Every line comes with its meaning, the Japanese and Ukrainian in their own scripts too, and the tricky words are spelled out the English way, with the stressed syllable in capitals.'],
                    ['Do I need to read music?', 'No. You hear each line from a guide and see your voice drawn as a line.'],
                    ['What does it cost?', 'Nothing: every course is free. Try the first lesson without an account; a free account opens the rest.'],
                ],
                'related' => ['diction', 'folk-and-early-music', 'breath-and-stamina'],
                'close' => 'Lesson 1 takes about three minutes: Sakura\'s first line on its vowels, then its words, then with the library\'s other parts.',
                // The love letter (docs/courses/other-languages-love-letter.md): only its facts, each with its notes. The
                // course's own facts, and the song courses', stay off this page. The old books' quotes were checked
                // against their scanned pages (Lord against the Project Gutenberg text); the rest is said without quotes.
                'love' => [
                    // The hero's picture: three city names, each four morae, in two, three and four syllables.
                    'map' => [
                        'words' => [
                            ['Tōkyō', 'とうきょう', ['to', 'u', 'kyo', 'u'], [2, 2]],
                            ['Ōsaka', 'おおさか', ['o', 'o', 'sa', 'ka'], [2, 1, 1]],
                            ['Nagasaki', 'ながさき', ['na', 'ga', 'sa', 'ki'], [1, 1, 1, 1]],
                        ],
                        'count' => ':n syllables',
                        'legend' => ['A mora: one beat of time', 'A syllable'],
                        'note' => 'Tōkyō, Ōsaka and Nagasaki all have four morae, though they have two, three and four syllables. Japanese times its words in morae, and a long vowel takes two.',
                    ],
                    'kicks' => [
                        'plain' => 'In plain words',
                        'archive' => 'From the archive',
                        'climb' => 'The course',
                        'ladder' => 'Unit 4',
                        'first' => 'Lesson 1',
                        'notes' => 'Margin notes',
                        'kit' => 'Before you begin',
                        'close' => 'Begin with one line',
                    ],
                    // The featured demo (js/public/other-languages.js): the pack's "One mora, one note".
                    'demo' => [
                        'live' => 'Live demo',
                        'tag' => 'No mic, just listen',
                        'title' => 'One mora, one note',
                        'lead' => 'In Japanese every small sound gets its own time. Here is "ka-gi-ri", from Sakura\'s third line, in the library\'s key: ka on G4, gi on G4 then E♭4, two notes on the one vowel, then ri on D4. Then merged, as "kag-ri", the way English would run it together.',
                        'how' => 'Then the same idea in a word: obasan, "aunt", four even morae, against obāsan, "grandmother", its ā held one mora longer. The length alone changes the word.',
                        'play' => 'Hear all three',
                        'stop' => 'Stop',
                        'ready' => 'Press play: ka-gi-ri as written, then merged, then obasan and obāsan.',
                        'one_label' => 'Or one at a time',
                        'buttons' => ['three' => 'Three morae', 'merged' => 'Merged', 'obasan' => 'Obasan, obāsan'],
                        'say' => [
                            'three' => 'Three morae: ka, gi, ri, each with its own start.',
                            'merged' => 'Merged, "kag-ri": no new start where gi should be.',
                            'obasan' => 'Obasan, "aunt": four morae. Obāsan, "grandmother": five, the ā held for two.',
                        ],
                        'after' => 'In the course the app scores exactly this: every syllable sung, on its own note and on the beat.',
                        'syllables' => [
                            'three' => ['ka', 'gi', 'ri'],
                            'merged' => ['kag', 'ri'],
                            'obasan' => ['o', 'ba', 'sa', 'n'],
                            'obaasan' => ['o', 'bā', 'sa', 'n'],
                        ],
                        'words' => ['obasan' => 'obasan, "aunt"', 'obaasan' => 'obāsan, "grandmother"'],
                        'notes' => ['G4' => 'G4', 'Eb4' => 'E♭4', 'D4' => 'D4', 'A4' => 'A4'],
                        'key' => ['A sung note', 'A fresh start', 'The n, hummed'],
                        'note' => 'Our synthesized voice: it sings the pitch and the timing, not the words, so the syllables are on screen. In the course the app hears your pitch and timing; your vowels are for your own ear.',
                    ],
                    'plain' => [
                        'title' => 'Every vowel, kept whole',
                        'glides' => 'Say "lay" slowly, then "low". Your tongue moves during the vowel: "lay" goes from "eh" towards "ee", "low" from "o" towards "oo". That is a diphthong, "a vowel with two different targets", and English is full of them: "no highway cowboys" has five. Japanese and Spanish each have five vowels, a, e, i, o, u, and Ukrainian six, and each is meant to hold still. On a held note a glide is easy to hear, because the vowel changes while the pitch stays put.',
                        'schwa' => 'English also shrinks its quiet syllables. Say "photograph", then "photography": the first o is full in one and shrinks in the other. The shrunk vowel is usually schwa, the "uh" sound: "Schwa, [ə], is the most common reduced vowel in English." Spanish keeps each of its five vowels in stressed and unstressed syllables alike. An English singer brings the habit along without noticing, so "sa-ku-ra" turns into "sa-ku-ruh".',
                        'asks_title' => 'Three things a new language asks for',
                        'asks' => [
                            ['O-o-sa-ka', 'Morae', 'Japanese times its words in morae. A mora is "the smallest unit of timing, equal to or shorter than a syllable": a short vowel is one, a long vowel two, and a final n is a mora of its own. The word is Latin, for "linger, delay".', ['mora', 'jphon']],
                            ['Si‿a', 'Sinalefa', 'Spanish and Italian run a word ending in a vowel into the next word starting with one: "the merging of two syllables into one, especially when it causes two words to be pronounced as one". In La Paloma, "Si a" shares one note.', ['synal']],
                            ['pero, perro', 'The tap', 'A tap is one quick flick of the tongue tip; a trill is several. Spanish tells words apart by it: "pero" (but) has a tap, "perro" (dog) a trill. Japanese r is most often a tap too, and Manuel Garcia\'s rule for the Italian r was "a single touch of the tongue against the teeth".', ['flap', 'jphon', 'garcia']],
                        ],
                    ],
                    // The second demo: Shchedryk's figure up the course's speed ladder, in tune or drifting flat.
                    'ladder' => [
                        'title' => 'Round and round, and still in key',
                        'lead' => 'Shchedryk is a traditional Ukrainian shchedrivka, a New Year song, whose tune Mykola Leontovych made famous in his 1916 choral setting; it\'s the tune many know as "Carol of the Bells". Unit 4 sings its four-note figure round and round, faster each time, and repeating a small figure fast, a choir can sag without noticing. Only a true note shows how far.',
                        'more' => 'The course climbs it at 60, 80 and 96, and never goes above 96. The library sings it at 132.',
                        'kick' => 'Hear the key',
                        'tag' => 'Shchedryk\'s figure',
                        'deck_title' => 'The figure, faster',
                        'deck_lead' => 'B♭4, A4, B♭4, G4: "Shche-dryk, shche-dryk". Twice at 60, then four times at 80 and four at 96, a beat\'s rest between rounds. Pick drifting flat and each round sags 5 cents more than the last. As it ends, a true G4 sounds with the last G.',
                        'play' => 'Climb the ladder',
                        'fast' => 'At 132, just listen',
                        'stop' => 'Stop',
                        'pick_label' => 'The voice',
                        'picks' => ['true' => 'In tune', 'flat' => 'Drifting flat'],
                        'syllables' => ['Shche-', 'dryk,', 'shche-', 'dryk,'],
                        'tempos' => ['60', '80', '96'],
                        'ready' => 'Press play and listen to the last G.',
                        'round' => 'Round :n of 10, at :bpm.',
                        'low' => ':c cents low.',
                        'end_true' => 'The last G against a true G4 at 392.00 Hz: one note, no beating.',
                        'end_flat' => 'The last G is 45 cents low, 381.94 Hz against a true 392.00 Hz. Hear them beat, about 10 times a second.',
                        'say_fast' => 'At 132, four times round, the library\'s tempo.',
                        'key' => ['A round, at its pitch', 'The true key'],
                        'note' => 'Our synthesized voice, not a singer. In the course the app hears this one: it scores whether you hold the key through the figure.',
                    ],
                    'archive' => [
                        'title' => 'Borrowed vowels, 1894 to 1961',
                        'more' => 'Four more, 1912 to 1961',
                        'rows' => [
                            ['1894', 'Boston', 'The ragged boys of Rome', 'Frances E. Lord, Professor of Latin at Wellesley College, published The Roman Pronunciation of Latin in 1894. She quoted the English Latin professor H. A. J. Munro on why Latin should take Italian\'s vowels: "The little ragged boys in the streets of Rome and Florence enunciate their vowels in a style of which princes might be proud." Her first instruction to her readers: "First of all pay particular attention to the vowel sounds, to make them full and distinct". And she gave the old Latin idea of time in speech: "a short vowel has the time of one beat (mora); a long vowel, of two beats".', ['lord']],
                            ['1894', 'London', 'Two vowels, one syllable', 'In Hints on Singing, Manuel Garcia gave his pupils a rule for an Italian line with more vowels than notes: "In Italian it happens frequently that syllables are in excess of the places assigned to them." The cure was "contracting two or more, as the case may be, into one syllable". His editor Hermann Klein added why English singers find it hard: "the Italian vowels are all single sounds", while the English ones mostly "consist of double sounds or diphthongs". His cure: "A good ear, refined utterance, and clever imitation".', ['garcia']],
                            ['1912', 'Philadelphia', 'DOE-MEE-NAY', 'Edward J. Murphy\'s Latin Pronounced for Singing printed the Vespers with every Latin word spelled out above it, so that anyone could sing it: Domine as "DOE-MEE-NAY", Gloria Patri as "GLO-REE-AH PAH-TREE", Amen as "AH-MANE". Its preface calls the Roman pronunciation, urged by Pope Pius X in a letter to the Archbishop of Bourges, "certainly the best for chant and singing", and quotes a complaint that "All the faults of English pronunciation are ingrafted on the Latin".', ['murphy']],
                            ['1913', 'London', 'Which language is that?', 'The soprano Nellie Melba gave a lecture on English diction at the Guildhall School of Music, which became the preface to Dora Duty Jones\'s Lyric Diction. Too often, she said, the words were so blurred "that it is difficult to decide in which particular language the singer is delivering his message." Singers, to her, were "the silver-voiced messengers of the poet and the musician".', ['melba']],
                            ['1914', 'Sheffield', 'Four choirs, one Latin', 'The chorus master Henry Coward put the problem plainly: "The chief difficulty is to get English choralists to sing Latin with the broad Italianized vowels." So he printed Beethoven\'s Mass with a respelling under each line, "Kee-ree-ay ay-lay-ee-zohn", and its English meaning under that: the model for four choirs, in Sheffield, Leeds, Huddersfield and Newcastle. The spelling was only a start. His singers would "easily learn to modify the broad sounds to the conductor\'s pattern", by ear.', ['coward']],
                            ['1961', 'The chant book', 'Read it before you sing it', 'The English edition of the Liber Usualis, the Church\'s book of chant, gives choirs its rules in a strict order: "No Choir should attempt to sing a melody before reading the text correctly and fluently." It admits that English has no exact match for the Latin vowels, so they are best learned by ear, and it lets no syllable shrink, so Domine never becomes Domne. The breath has its Golden Rule: "Never take breath just before a fresh syllable of a word".', ['liber']],
                        ],
                    ],
                    'facts_title' => 'Small things worth knowing',
                    'facts_more' => 'Four more',
                    'facts' => [
                        ['A haiku\'s 5, 7 and 5 are counted in morae, not syllables.', 'Wikipedia, Mora (linguistics)', ['mora']],
                        ['Even Japan\'s own name changes length: Nihon has three morae, Nippon four.', 'Wikipedia, Mora (linguistics)', ['mora']],
                        ['A Japanese long vowel lasts about 2.5 to 3 times as long as a short one.', 'Wikipedia, Japanese phonology', ['jphon']],
                        ['Many Americans already make the tap: it\'s the t in "metal" when it sounds like "medal".', 'Wikipedia, Flap consonant', ['flap']],
                        ['Church Latin keeps apart the vowels that Spanish would join: diei is sung di-e-i, and filii fi-li-i.', 'The Liber Usualis (1961 printing)', ['liber']],
                        ['Standard Ukrainian keeps an unstressed o as an o. Turning it towards a, called akanye, belongs to some northern dialects, Polissian and Slobozhan.', 'Wikipedia, Ukrainian phonology and Akanye', ['uphon', 'akanye']],
                    ],
                    // The sources (docs/courses/other-languages-love-letter.md), numbered in this order.
                    'sources' => [
                        'mora' => ['Wikipedia, Mora (linguistics)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mora_(linguistics)'],
                        'jphon' => ['Wikipedia, Japanese phonology', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_phonology'],
                        'diph' => ['Wikipedia, Diphthong', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diphthong'],
                        'sphon' => ['Wikipedia, Spanish phonology', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanish_phonology'],
                        'uphon' => ['Wikipedia, Ukrainian phonology', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukrainian_phonology'],
                        'stress' => ['Wikipedia, Stress and vowel reduction in English', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stress_and_vowel_reduction_in_English'],
                        'synal' => ['Wikipedia, Synalepha', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synalepha'],
                        'flap' => ['Wikipedia, Flap consonant', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flap_consonant'],
                        'lord' => ['Frances E. Lord, The Roman Pronunciation of Latin (Boston, Ginn, 1894), Project Gutenberg', 'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7528'],
                        'garcia' => ['Manuel Garcia, Hints on Singing (1894; ed. Hermann Klein): pages 46, 49 and 50', 'https://archive.org/details/hintsonsinging00garc'],
                        'murphy' => ['Edward J. Murphy, Latin Pronounced for Singing (Philadelphia, 1912): the preface and page 3', 'https://archive.org/details/cu31924021787332'],
                        'melba' => ['Dora Duty Jones, Lyric Diction for Singers, Actors and Public Speakers (1913), preface by Nellie Melba: pages xii and xiii', 'https://archive.org/details/lyricdictionfors00jone'],
                        'coward' => ['Henry Coward, Choral Technique and Interpretation (1914): Appendix III, page 308', 'https://archive.org/details/choraltechniquei00cowa'],
                        'liber' => ['The Liber Usualis (1961 printing), Rules for Interpretation: pages xxxv to xxxix', 'https://archive.org/details/TheLiberUsualis1961'],
                        'akanye' => ['Wikipedia, Akanye', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akanye'],
